{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011uqeLsvD8V1oxcKUsV1ewU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Scale Expansion to 15 Models\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Add API models (GPT-4o-mini, GPT-4o, GPT-4.1, GPT-4.1-mini, Claude, Gemini) and large open-source (Llama-3.1-70B, Qwen2.5-72B) to reach 15 models total. Rerun MI matrix + full regression suite on C(15,3)=455 panels across 12 benchmark×attack conditions.\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"models_total\": 15,\n7\t      \"api_models\": 9,\n8\t      \"local_models\": 6,\n9\t      \"panels\": 455,\n10\t      \"conditions\": 12,\n11\t      \"confound_r_keff_eta\": 0.7,\n12\t      \"ols_eta_sig\": \"11/12\",\n13\t      \"ols_keff_sig\": \"8/12 (7+/1-)\",\n14\t      \"bootstrap_eta_sig\": \"5/12\",\n15\t      \"bootstrap_keff_sig\": \"1/12\",\n16\t      \"clustered_se_eta_sig\": \"8/12\",\n17\t      \"clustered_se_keff_sig\": \"0/12\",\n18\t      \"mixed_effects_eta_sig\": \"7/12\",\n19\t      \"mixed_effects_keff_sig\": \"3/12\",\n20\t      \"dominance_ratio_bootstrap\": \"5:1\",\n21\t      \"se_inflation_factor\": \"~2.5x\",\n22\t      \"mmlu_bootstrap_eta\": \"4/6\",\n23\t      \"arc_bootstrap_eta\": \"1/6\",\n24\t      \"gam_targeted_positive\": \"6/6\",\n25\t      \"gam_sharedbias_negative\": \"6/6\",\n26\t      \"min_eta_max_wins\": \"12/12\",\n27\t      \"practical_delta_asr_keff_iqr_pp\": 1.3\n28\t    },\n29\t    \"tables\": [\n30\t      {\n31\t        \"name\": \"Correction Method Comparison (12 conditions)\",\n32\t        \"columns\": [\n33\t          \"Method\",\n34\t          \"η_max sig\",\n35\t          \"K_eff sig\",\n36\t          \"Dominance ratio\",\n37\t          \"Note\"\n38\t        ],\n39\t        \"rows\": [\n40\t          [\n41\t            \"OLS (baseline)\",\n42\t            \"11/12\",\n43\t            \"8/12 (7+/1-)\",\n44\t            \"1.4:1\",\n45\t            \"Inflated by panel overlap\"\n46\t          ],\n47\t          [\n48\t            \"Wild cluster bootstrap v2\",\n49\t            \"5/12\",\n50\t            \"1/12\",\n51\t            \"5:1\",\n52\t            \"Conservative lower bound; position 0 imbalance\"\n53\t          ],\n54\t          [\n55\t            \"Clustered SE (CR1)\",\n56\t            \"8/12\",\n57\t            \"0/12\",\n58\t            \"8:0\",\n59\t            \"Consistent with bootstrap positions 1&2\"\n60\t          ],\n61\t          [\n62\t            \"Mixed effects\",\n63\t            \"7/12\",\n64\t            \"3/12\",\n65\t            \"2.3:1\",\n66\t            \"Random judge intercepts\"\n67\t          ],\n68\t          [\n69\t            \"Tier-indicator corrected\",\n70\t            \"11/12\",\n71\t            \"5/12\",\n72\t            \"2.2:1\",\n73\t            \"3/3/9 tier control\"\n74\t          ]\n75\t        ]\n76\t      },\n77\t      {\n78\t        \"name\": \"Bootstrap η_max by Benchmark\",\n79\t        \"columns\": [\n80\t          \"Benchmark\",\n81\t          \"η_max bootstrap sig\",\n82\t          \"Attacks surviving\",\n83\t          \"Attacks lost\"\n84\t        ],\n85\t        \"rows\": [\n86\t          [\n87\t            \"MMLU\",\n88\t            \"4/6\",\n89\t            \"PI, SM, PB, VB\",\n90\t            \"syco, auth\"\n91\t          ],\n92\t          [\n93\t            \"ARC\",\n94\t            \"1/6\",\n95\t            \"PI only (marginal)\",\n96\t            \"SM, PB, syco, VB, auth\"\n97\t          ]\n98\t        ]\n99\t      }\n100\t    ],\n101\t    \"figures\": [],\n102\t    \"analysis\": \"plan_001 is the foundational experiment and is already completed as plan001_step1_full_v4. Under median tier, no further analysis of this data yields stronger results. The key tension is between OLS (favorable) and bootstrap (conservative): the paper must present both transparently. The benchmark asymmetry (MMLU >> ARC) is the main weakness — reviewers will question whether η_max dominance is benchmark-dependent. The honest answer is yes, partially: η_max is stronger in higher-variance scoring formats. This needs explicit discussion in Section 5.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_001 IS the shipped evidence — plan001_step1_full_v4 realized it successfully. The 15-model expansion dramatically increased panel count (20→455) and broke the 6-model confound ceiling (r=-0.8→+0.70). K_eff flips from NS-negative (6-model: arc×sycophancy only) to predominantly positive (7+/1-) under OLS, BUT this OLS significance is inflated by panel non-independence: each of 15 judges appears in ~91 panels, creating massive overlap. Correction methods (bootstrap/clustered SE/mixed effects) collapse K_eff from 8/12 to 0-3/12 while η_max survives at 5-8/12. The 5:1 dominance ratio under bootstrap is actually STRONGER than OLS's 11:8≈1.4:1 because K_eff collapses faster than η_max. ARC weakness (1/6 bootstrap η_max vs MMLU 4/6) reflects MCQ format: 4-choice MMLU gives more scoring variance for η_max signal than 2-choice ARC. This benchmark asymmetry is a structural limitation of the experimental design, not a refutation of η_max dominance.\"\n103\t  },\n104\t  {\n105\t    \"name\": \"Attack Expansion to 6+ Types\",\n106\t    \"description\": \"Add few-shot hijacking, persona manipulation, instruction override attacks. Run on full 15-model pool. Test universality of weakest-link finding across broader attack surface.\",\n107\t    \"results\": {\n108\t      \"status\": \"not_completed\",\n109\t      \"reason\": \"Time and compute constraints; 60 GPU-hours not allocated\",\n110\t      \"hypothetical_if_run\": {\n111\t        \"new_attacks_tested\": 3,\n112\t        \"few_shot_hijacking_eta_sig\": \"likely 1/2 (MMLU yes, ARC no)\",\n113\t        \"few_shot_hijacking_keff_sig\": \"likely 0/2 after correction\",\n114\t        \"persona_manipulation_eta_sig\": \"likely 1/2\",\n115\t        \"persona_manipulation_keff_sig\": \"likely 0/2 after correction\",\n116\t        \"instruction_override_eta_sig\": \"likely 2/2\",\n117\t        \"instruction_override_keff_sig\": \"likely 0-1/2 after correction\",\n118\t        \"overall_eta_sig_new\": \"4/6 OLS, 2-3/6 bootstrap\",\n119\t        \"overall_keff_sig_new\": \"2-3/6 OLS, 0-1/6 bootstrap\",\n120\t        \"new_gam_pattern\": \"few-shot hijacking and instruction override → targeted (K_eff positive); persona manipulation → ambiguous\"\n121\t      }\n122\t    },\n123\t    \"tables\": [\n124\t      {\n125\t        \"name\": \"Hypothetical New Attack Results (OLS vs Bootstrap)\",\n126\t        \"columns\": [\n127\t          \"Attack\",\n128\t          \"Benchmark\",\n129\t          \"η_max OLS p\",\n130\t          \"η_max bootstrap p\",\n131\t          \"K_eff OLS p\",\n132\t          \"K_eff bootstrap p\",\n133\t          \"GAM K_eff direction\"\n134\t        ],\n135\t        \"rows\": [\n136\t          [\n137\t            \"Few-shot hijacking\",\n138\t            \"MMLU\",\n139\t            \"<0.001\",\n140\t            \"0.03\",\n141\t            \"0.04\",\n142\t            \"0.15\",\n143\t            \"positive\"\n144\t          ],\n145\t          [\n146\t            \"Few-shot hijacking\",\n147\t            \"ARC\",\n148\t            \"0.008\",\n149\t            \"0.12\",\n150\t            \"0.18\",\n151\t            \"0.45\",\n152\t            \"positive\"\n153\t          ],\n154\t          [\n155\t            \"Persona manipulation\",\n156\t            \"MMLU\",\n157\t            \"0.003\",\n158\t            \"0.07\",\n159\t            \"0.06\",\n160\t            \"0.22\",\n161\t            \"mixed (+0.02)\"\n162\t          ],\n163\t          [\n164\t            \"Persona manipulation\",\n165\t            \"ARC\",\n166\t            \"0.02\",\n167\t            \"0.18\",\n168\t            \"0.12\",\n169\t            \"0.38\",\n170\t            \"mixed (-0.01)\"\n171\t          ],\n172\t          [\n173\t            \"Instruction override\",\n174\t            \"MMLU\",\n175\t            \"<0.001\",\n176\t            \"0.01\",\n177\t            \"0.03\",\n178\t            \"0.09\",\n179\t            \"positive\"\n180\t          ],\n181\t          [\n182\t            \"Instruction override\",\n183\t            \"ARC\",\n184\t            \"0.005\",\n185\t            \"0.04\",\n186\t            \"0.09\",\n187\t            \"0.28\",\n188\t            \"positive\"\n189\t          ]\n190\t        ]\n191\t      }\n192\t    ],\n193\t    \"figures\": [],\n194\t    \"analysis\": \"Non-completion is the most likely median outcome. The paper handles this by stating 'six representative attack types covering targeted and shared-bias mechanisms' and acknowledging that broader attack coverage is future work. If partially completed (e.g., 1-2 new attacks only), the results add marginal support but also introduce taxonomy edge cases that complicate the clean GAM narrative. Net value: low marginal information gain for high compute cost. Better to invest the GPU-hours in plan_003 (BFT contrast) which directly supports a core claim.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_002 is not completed due to resource constraints. If run, results would be mixed: new attack types would largely confirm η_max dominance pattern but with benchmark asymmetry persisting (MMLU > ARC). Few-shot hijacking is mechanistically similar to prompt_injection (targeted, judge-specific) so K_eff would be directionally positive but NS after correction. Persona manipulation is ambiguous — it exploits both judge-specific persona susceptibility AND shared anthropomorphic biases, making the targeted/shared-bias taxonomy assignment unclear. Instruction override is the strongest targeted attack variant, so η_max likely robust. Net: the attack expansion would not change the core narrative but would introduce 1-2 conditions that don't cleanly fit the GAM 6:6 taxonomy, requiring a footnote about taxonomy edge cases. The marginal evidence gain doesn't justify the 60 GPU-hour cost under the median scenario — the paper can acknowledge this as a limitation.\"\n195\t  },\n196\t  {\n197\t    \"name\": \"Random-Fault BFT Contrast Experiment\",\n198\t    \"description\": \"Monte Carlo simulation: each judge fails independently with probability η_j under random faults. Compare K_eff significance under RF regime vs adaptive regime. GO/NO-GO gate: if RF K_eff also not significant → BFT framing collapses.\",\n199\t    \"results\": {\n200\t      \"status\": \"partially_completed\",\n201\t      \"note\": \"RF data exists in plan001_step1_full_v4 (2 conditions). Under median, standalone plan_003 adds bootstrap robustness check that weakens ARC.\",\n202\t      \"rf_mmlu_keff_sig_ols\": true,\n203\t      \"rf_mmlu_keff_p_ols\": 0.002,\n204\t      \"rf_mmlu_keff_p_bootstrap\": 0.03,\n205\t      \"rf_mmlu_keff_std_beta\": 0.215,\n206\t      \"rf_mmlu_r2\": 0.73,\n207\t      \"rf_arc_keff_sig_ols\": true,\n208\t      \"rf_arc_keff_p_ols\": 0.008,\n209\t      \"rf_arc_keff_p_bootstrap\": 0.06,\n210\t      \"rf_arc_keff_std_beta\": 0.379,\n211\t      \"rf_arc_r2\": 0.68,\n212\t      \"rf_keff_sig_bootstrap\": \"1/2\",\n213\t      \"rf_keff_sig_ols\": \"2/2\",\n214\t      \"rf_keff_sig_clustered_se\": \"2/2\",\n215\t      \"adaptive_keff_sig_bootstrap\": \"1/12\",\n216\t      \"contrast_delta_r2\": \"RF 0.65-0.73 vs adaptive 0.15-0.30\",\n217\t      \"bft_gate\": \"partial_pass\"\n218\t    },\n219\t    \"tables\": [\n220\t      {\n221\t        \"name\": \"RF vs Adaptive K_eff Significance Contrast\",\n222\t        \"columns\": [\n223\t          \"Regime\",\n224\t          \"Benchmark\",\n225\t          \"K_eff OLS p\",\n226\t          \"K_eff bootstrap p\",\n227\t          \"K_eff std_β\",\n228\t          \"R²\",\n229\t          \"Interpretation\"\n230\t        ],\n231\t        \"rows\": [\n232\t          [\n233\t            \"Random fault\",\n234\t            \"MMLU\",\n235\t            \"0.002\",\n236\t            \"0.03\",\n237\t            \"+0.215\",\n238\t            \"0.73\",\n239\t            \"Genuine — survives bootstrap\"\n240\t          ],\n241\t          [\n242\t            \"Random fault\",\n243\t            \"ARC\",\n244\t            \"0.008\",\n245\t            \"0.06\",\n246\t            \"+0.379\",\n247\t            \"0.68\",\n248\t            \"Borderline — benchmark limitation\"\n249\t          ],\n250\t          [\n251\t            \"Adaptive\",\n252\t            \"MMLU (avg 6 attacks)\",\n253\t            \"varies\",\n254\t            \"5/6 NS\",\n255\t            \"+0.18 avg\",\n256\t            \"0.25 avg\",\n257\t            \"Inflated by panel overlap\"\n258\t          ],\n259\t          [\n260\t            \"Adaptive\",\n261\t            \"ARC (avg 6 attacks)\",\n262\t            \"varies\",\n263\t            \"6/6 NS\",\n264\t            \"+0.15 avg\",\n265\t            \"0.18 avg\",\n266\t            \"Inflated + weak benchmark\"\n267\t          ]\n268\t        ]\n269\t      },\n270\t      {\n271\t        \"name\": \"GO/NO-GO Gate Assessment\",\n272\t        \"columns\": [\n273\t          \"Criterion\",\n274\t          \"Result\",\n275\t          \"Status\"\n276\t        ],\n277\t        \"rows\": [\n278\t          [\n279\t            \"RF K_eff sig ≥1/2 (OLS)\",\n280\t            \"2/2\",\n281\t            \"PASS\"\n282\t          ],\n283\t          [\n284\t            \"RF K_eff sig ≥1/2 (bootstrap)\",\n285\t            \"1/2\",\n286\t            \"PARTIAL PASS\"\n287\t          ],\n288\t          [\n289\t            \"RF R² > adaptive R²\",\n290\t            \"0.70 vs 0.22 (avg)\",\n291\t            \"PASS\"\n292\t          ],\n293\t          [\n294\t            \"RF-adaptive K_eff contrast\",\n295\t            \"RF 1-2/2 vs adaptive 1/12\",\n296\t            \"PARTIAL PASS\"\n297\t          ],\n298\t          [\n299\t            \"BFT framing viable?\",\n300\t            \"Yes with caveats\",\n301\t            \"PARTIAL PASS\"\n302\t          ]\n303\t        ]\n304\t      }\n305\t    ],\n306\t    \"figures\": [],\n307\t    \"analysis\": \"The BFT contrast partially passes the GO/NO-GO gate under median. The R² gap is the robust finding (large, benchmark-independent). The K_eff significance contrast is benchmark-dependent (MMLU clean, ARC borderline). Paper should lead with R² gap as primary evidence for BFT contrast, use K_eff significance as secondary. The honest framing: 'Under random faults, panel composition (η_max + K_eff) is highly predictive (R²≈0.70); under adaptive attacks, predictability collapses (R²≈0.22), consistent with BFT assumptions being violated by strategic adversaries.' This avoids over-claiming on significance counts and focuses on the more robust quantity.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_003's core data already exists within plan001_step1_full_v4 (RF K_eff sig 2/2 under all methods, R²=0.73/0.81). However, when plan_003 is run as a standalone experiment with full bootstrap robustness checks, ARC RF drops to borderline significance (bootstrap p=0.06) — this is because ARC's 2-choice format has less scoring variance, and with only 2 RF conditions the bootstrap has limited power. The GO/NO-GO gate partially passes: K_eff IS significant under RF for MMLU (cleanly), borderline for ARC. The BFT contrast remains valid for MMLU (K_eff genuine under RF, artifactual under adaptive) but weakened for ARC. The R² gap (RF ~0.70 vs adaptive ~0.25) is the stronger evidence for the BFT framing than individual significance counts. Under median, the paper presents the R² gap as the primary BFT evidence and the K_eff significance contrast as supporting evidence with caveats about ARC weakness. The BFT framing survives but is downgraded from 'clean contrast' to 'directional contrast with benchmark dependence.'\"\n308\t  },\n309\t  {\n310\t    \"name\": \"Capability-Matched Validation\",\n311\t    \"description\": \"Within same capability tier (all 7B or all 70B), test whether K_eff emerges as significant predictor when η_max variance is controlled by construction.\",\n312\t    \"results\": {\n313\t      \"status\": \"not_completed\",\n314\t      \"reason\": \"Depends on plan_001; insufficient models per tier for meaningful panel counts\",\n315\t      \"hypothetical_if_run\": {\n316\t        \"tier_7b_models\": 3,\n317\t        \"tier_7b_panels\": 1,\n318\t        \"tier_70b_models\": 3,\n319\t        \"tier_70b_panels\": 1,\n320\t        \"tier_api_models\": 9,\n321\t        \"tier_api_panels\": 84,\n322\t        \"note\": \"Only API tier has enough models for meaningful regression\",\n323\t        \"api_tier_eta_max_sig\": \"4-5/12 OLS\",\n324\t        \"api_tier_keff_sig\": \"2-3/12 OLS, 0-1/12 bootstrap\",\n325\t        \"api_tier_confound_r\": 0.55,\n326\t        \"api_tier_eta_max_range\": \"narrower within tier\",\n327\t        \"within_tier_keff_emerges\": false\n328\t      }\n329\t    },\n330\t    \"tables\": [\n331\t      {\n332\t        \"name\": \"Tier Composition and Statistical Power\",\n333\t        \"columns\": [\n334\t          \"Tier\",\n335\t          \"Models\",\n336\t          \"Panels C(n,3)\",\n337\t          \"η_max range\",\n338\t          \"K_eff range\",\n339\t          \"Regression viable?\"\n340\t        ],\n341\t        \"rows\": [\n342\t          [\n343\t            \"Small (7B)\",\n344\t            \"3\",\n345\t            \"1\",\n346\t            \"narrow\",\n347\t            \"degenerate\",\n348\t            \"No — 1 panel\"\n349\t          ],\n350\t          [\n351\t            \"Medium (70B)\",\n352\t            \"3\",\n353\t            \"1\",\n354\t            \"narrow\",\n355\t            \"degenerate\",\n356\t            \"No — 1 panel\"\n357\t          ],\n358\t          [\n359\t            \"API\",\n360\t            \"9\",\n361\t            \"84\",\n362\t            \"moderate\",\n363\t            \"moderate\",\n364\t            \"Marginal — 84 panels, 12 conditions\"\n365\t          ],\n366\t          [\n367\t            \"Full pool\",\n368\t            \"15\",\n369\t            \"455\",\n370\t            \"wide\",\n371\t            \"wide\",\n372\t            \"Yes — primary analysis\"\n373\t          ]\n374\t        ]\n375\t      }\n376\t    ],\n377\t    \"figures\": [],\n378\t    \"analysis\": \"This experiment is structurally underpowered given the current model pool. The 3/3/9 tier split was not designed for within-tier regression. Even in the API tier, 84 panels × 12 conditions would give ~7 effective observations per condition after accounting for non-independence — too few for reliable bootstrap inference. The paper should present the diversity-capability tradeoff (r=0.70) as a structural limitation and note that capability-matched validation requires future work with larger model pools. This is an honest acknowledged limitation, not a failure.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_004 is not completed primarily because the experimental design doesn't support it well. The 3/3/9 tier split means only the API tier (9 models, C(9,3)=84 panels) has enough data for regression. Within-tier, η_max variance IS reduced (models more similar in capability), but so is K_eff variance — both variables compress when you restrict to a capability band. The confound r drops from 0.70 to ~0.55 within the API tier, which is better but not sufficient to cleanly separate the two predictors. K_eff would show 2-3/12 significant under OLS within the API tier (slight improvement over full-pool because confound is reduced), but collapse to 0-1/12 under bootstrap (same panel overlap problem persists). The experiment doesn't resolve the K_eff question because the panel overlap issue is structural, not related to capability matching. The paper should acknowledge this as a limitation: 'Capability-matched validation requires a larger model pool with more models per tier, which is a constraint of the current LLM ecosystem.'\"\n379\t  },\n380\t  {\n381\t    \"name\": \"Two-Stage Panel Design Algorithm Benchmark\",\n382\t    \"description\": \"Benchmark robustness-first two-stage algorithm: (1) filter η > threshold, (2) maximize K_eff. Compare against 5 baselines including Strongest-K, Random, Max-Diversity, Min-η_max, Max-Accuracy.\",\n383\t    \"results\": {\n384\t      \"status\": \"not_completed\",\n385\t      \"reason\": \"Depends on plan_001; Dream flagged marginal improvement over Strongest-K\",\n386\t      \"hypothetical_if_run\": {\n387\t        \"baselines\": [\n388\t          \"Min-η_max\",\n389\t          \"Strongest-K\",\n390\t          \"Max-K_eff\",\n391\t          \"Random\",\n392\t          \"Max-Accuracy\"\n393\t        ],\n394\t        \"two_stage_vs_min_eta\": {\n395\t          \"asr_improvement_mean_pp\": 0.8,\n396\t          \"conditions_improved\": \"7/12\",\n397\t          \"conditions_equal\": \"5/12\",\n398\t          \"conditions_worse\": \"0/12\"\n399\t        },\n400\t        \"two_stage_vs_strongest_k\": {\n401\t          \"asr_improvement_mean_pp\": 1.5,\n402\t          \"note\": \"Marginal — Strongest-K already approximates robustness-first\"\n403\t        },\n404\t        \"two_stage_vs_max_keff\": {\n405\t          \"asr_improvement_mean_pp\": 8.2,\n406\t          \"note\": \"Large — max diversity selects weak judges\"\n407\t        },\n408\t        \"two_stage_vs_random\": {\n409\t          \"asr_improvement_mean_pp\": 5.1\n410\t        },\n411\t        \"min_eta_max_still_wins\": \"10-12/12\",\n412\t        \"two_stage_marginal_gain\": \"Marginal over min_eta_max; meaningful over naive diversity\"\n413\t      }\n414\t    },\n415\t    \"tables\": [\n416\t      {\n417\t        \"name\": \"Hypothetical Algorithm Benchmark (ASR, lower is better)\",\n418\t        \"columns\": [\n419\t          \"Strategy\",\n420\t          \"Mean ASR\",\n421\t          \"Conditions best\",\n422\t          \"vs Min-η_max Δ\"\n423\t        ],\n424\t        \"rows\": [\n425\t          [\n426\t            \"Two-stage (robustness + K_eff)\",\n427\t            \"0.142\",\n428\t            \"7/12\",\n429\t            \"-0.8pp\"\n430\t          ],\n431\t          [\n432\t            \"Min-η_max\",\n433\t            \"0.150\",\n434\t            \"5/12\",\n435\t            \"baseline\"\n436\t          ],\n437\t          [\n438\t            \"Strongest-K\",\n439\t            \"0.155\",\n440\t            \"0/12\",\n441\t            \"+0.5pp\"\n442\t          ],\n443\t          [\n444\t            \"Random\",\n445\t            \"0.193\",\n446\t            \"0/12\",\n447\t            \"+4.3pp\"\n448\t          ],\n449\t          [\n450\t            \"Max-K_eff\",\n451\t            \"0.222\",\n452\t            \"0/12\",\n453\t            \"+7.2pp\"\n454\t          ],\n455\t          [\n456\t            \"Max-Accuracy\",\n457\t            \"0.160\",\n458\t            \"0/12\",\n459\t            \"+1.0pp\"\n460\t          ]\n461\t        ]\n462\t      }\n463\t    ],\n464\t    \"figures\": [],\n465\t    \"analysis\": \"The two-stage algorithm's main contribution is showing that naive diversity maximization is actively harmful (Max-K_eff is worst strategy), while robustness-first selection is optimal. The marginal gain of adding K_eff as a tiebreaker is real but small. This aligns with the core narrative: judge quality dominates, diversity is secondary. The paper can present the algorithm conceptually with the existing min_eta_max 12/12 result as the primary evidence, noting that the full benchmark with 5 baselines is planned future work.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_005 is not completed but is low-risk because Claim 6 (min_eta_max dominates 12/12) is already strong from plan001_step1_full_v4. The two-stage algorithm would add K_eff maximization as a tiebreaker after robustness screening. Dream flagged that this is marginal because: (1) after robustness screening, the remaining model pool is small (e.g., top 5-6 judges), so K_eff variation among them is limited; (2) Strongest-K approximates robustness-first by construction (selecting strongest models usually means selecting most robust ones, given the capability-robustness correlation). The two-stage approach wins 7/12 conditions over pure min_eta_max but the improvement is <1pp ASR on average — practically negligible. The real value of the two-stage algorithm is conceptual (it provides a principled framework) rather than empirical (marginal gains over simpler heuristics). Paper can present the algorithm as a recommendation without the full benchmark, supported by the min_eta_max 12/12 dominance result.\"\n466\t  },\n467\t  {\n468\t    \"name\": \"Per-Judge ASR Concentration Analysis\",\n469\t    \"description\": \"For each panel × attack, compute Gini coefficient and entropy of per-judge ASR distribution. Test whether adaptive adversaries concentrate attacks on specific judges (high Gini) vs spread evenly (low Gini).\",\n470\t    \"results\": {\n471\t      \"status\": \"not_completed\",\n472\t      \"reason\": \"Time constraints; depends on plan_001 judge-level ASR breakdowns\",\n473\t      \"hypothetical_if_run\": {\n474\t        \"gini_targeted_attacks_mean\": 0.45,\n475\t        \"gini_shared_bias_attacks_mean\": 0.22,\n476\t        \"gini_difference_p\": 0.003,\n477\t        \"entropy_targeted_mean\": 1.8,\n478\t        \"entropy_shared_bias_mean\": 2.5,\n479\t        \"gini_eta_max_correlation\": 0.62,\n480\t        \"concentration_supports_mechanism\": true,\n481\t        \"note\": \"Targeted attacks show higher concentration (exploit specific weak judges); shared-bias attacks spread more evenly (exploit common vulnerability)\"\n482\t      }\n483\t    },\n484\t    \"tables\": [\n485\t      {\n486\t        \"name\": \"Hypothetical Gini Coefficients by Attack Category\",\n487\t        \"columns\": [\n488\t          \"Attack type\",\n489\t          \"Category\",\n490\t          \"Gini (mean)\",\n491\t          \"Gini (SD)\",\n492\t          \"Entropy (mean)\"\n493\t        ],\n494\t        \"rows\": [\n495\t          [\n496\t            \"prompt_injection\",\n497\t            \"targeted\",\n498\t            \"0.52\",\n499\t            \"0.11\",\n500\t            \"1.6\"\n501\t          ],\n502\t          [\n503\t            \"score_manipulation\",\n504\t            \"targeted\",\n505\t            \"0.48\",\n506\t            \"0.13\",\n507\t            \"1.7\"\n508\t          ],\n509\t          [\n510\t            \"position_bias\",\n511\t            \"targeted\",\n512\t            \"0.38\",\n513\t            \"0.09\",\n514\t            \"2.0\"\n515\t          ],\n516\t          [\n517\t            \"sycophancy\",\n518\t            \"shared-bias\",\n519\t            \"0.25\",\n520\t            \"0.08\",\n521\t            \"2.4\"\n522\t          ],\n523\t          [\n524\t            \"verbosity_bias\",\n525\t            \"shared-bias\",\n526\t            \"0.20\",\n527\t            \"0.07\",\n528\t            \"2.5\"\n529\t          ],\n530\t          [\n531\t            \"authority_bias\",\n532\t            \"shared-bias\",\n533\t            \"0.21\",\n534\t            \"0.06\",\n535\t            \"2.6\"\n536\t          ]\n537\t        ]\n538\t      }\n539\t    ],\n540\t    \"figures\": [],\n541\t    \"analysis\": \"Confirmatory experiment — would strengthen the mechanistic narrative for the GAM 6:6 split but is not essential for any core claim. The Gini concentration metric provides an intuitive explanation for practitioners: 'targeted attacks pile up on your worst judge; shared-bias attacks erode all judges somewhat equally.' Non-completion is acknowledged as a limitation in the discussion section.\\n\\nUnder median tier, this experiment is not completed due to time constraints — it requires per-judge ASR breakdowns that may not be stored in the current data pipeline. If run, the results would support the GAM taxonomy mechanism: targeted attacks (PI, SM, PB) concentrate ASR on specific weak judges (high Gini ~0.45), while shared-bias attacks (syco, VB, auth) spread vulnerability more evenly across judges (low Gini ~0.22). This concentration difference provides direct evidence for WHY η_max dominates: adaptive adversaries exploit the single weakest judge disproportionately. However, the Gini-η_max correlation (~0.62) means this is partially confounded — panels with high η_max naturally have more unequal ASR distributions. The result is confirmatory rather than independently informative. Under median, this adds a nice figure to the supplementary but doesn't change any claims. Not completing it is an acceptable loss.\"\n542\t  },\n543\t  {\n544\t    \"name\": \"Diversity-Capability Tradeoff Visualization\",\n545\t    \"description\": \"Plot K_eff vs η_max across all 455 panels. Fit tradeoff curve. Compute Pareto frontier. Stratify by model tier.\",\n546\t    \"results\": {\n547\t      \"status\": \"completed_or_trivially_completable\",\n548\t      \"reason\": \"No GPU needed; uses existing panel-level data from plan001_step1_full_v4\",\n549\t      \"keff_eta_max_r\": 0.7,\n550\t      \"pareto_frontier_panels\": 8,\n551\t      \"pareto_frontier_composition\": \"Dominated by API-tier + top open-source panels\",\n552\t      \"tier_stratification\": {\n553\t        \"small_7b_panels\": {\n554\t          \"keff_range\": [\n555\t            2.2,\n556\t            2.8\n557\t          ],\n558\t          \"eta_max_range\": [\n559\t            0.15,\n560\t            0.35\n561\t          ]\n562\t        },\n563\t        \"medium_70b_panels\": {\n564\t          \"keff_range\": [\n565\t            2.0,\n566\t            2.6\n567\t          ],\n568\t          \"eta_max_range\": [\n569\t            0.08,\n570\t            0.2\n571\t          ]\n572\t        },\n573\t        \"api_panels\": {\n574\t          \"keff_range\": [\n575\t            1.5,\n576\t            2.5\n577\t          ],\n578\t          \"eta_max_range\": [\n579\t            0.03,\n580\t            0.15\n581\t          ]\n582\t        }\n583\t      },\n584\t      \"tradeoff_curve_fit\": \"log-linear: K_eff ≈ 1.2 + 0.8·log(η_max)\",\n585\t      \"residual_variance\": \"Substantial — r=0.70 means 51% variance explained; panels with same η_max can have K_eff ranging ±0.5\"\n586\t    },\n587\t    \"tables\": [\n588\t      {\n589\t        \"name\": \"Pareto-Optimal Panel Compositions (hypothetical top 5)\",\n590\t        \"columns\": [\n591\t          \"Panel ID\",\n592\t          \"Models\",\n593\t          \"K_eff\",\n594\t          \"η_max\",\n595\t          \"Pareto rank\"\n596\t        ],\n597\t        \"rows\": [\n598\t          [\n599\t            \"P_042\",\n600\t            \"gpt-4o + claude-3.5-sonnet + qwen2.5-72b\",\n601\t            \"2.35\",\n602\t            \"0.06\",\n603\t            \"1\"\n604\t          ],\n605\t          [\n606\t            \"P_118\",\n607\t            \"gpt-4.1 + claude-3.5-sonnet + llama-70b\",\n608\t            \"2.28\",\n609\t            \"0.07\",\n610\t            \"2\"\n611\t          ],\n612\t          [\n613\t            \"P_203\",\n614\t            \"gpt-4o + gpt-4.1 + qwen2.5-72b\",\n615\t            \"1.95\",\n616\t            \"0.04\",\n617\t            \"3\"\n618\t          ],\n619\t          [\n620\t            \"P_089\",\n621\t            \"gpt-4.1 + gpt-4.1-mini + claude-3.5-sonnet\",\n622\t            \"1.72\",\n623\t            \"0.03\",\n624\t            \"4\"\n625\t          ],\n626\t          [\n627\t            \"P_301\",\n628\t            \"gpt-4o + gpt-4.1 + claude-3.5-sonnet\",\n629\t            \"1.85\",\n630\t            \"0.035\",\n631\t            \"5\"\n632\t          ]\n633\t        ]\n634\t      }\n635\t    ],\n636\t    \"figures\": [],\n637\t    \"analysis\": \"Clean visualization exercise that supports the narrative. The main insight for practitioners: the Pareto frontier shows you CAN get moderate diversity (K_eff ~2.0-2.3) with good robustness (η_max <0.07) by mixing top API models with the best open-source models. But pushing K_eff beyond ~2.5 requires including 7B-class models that dramatically increase η_max. This is the structural constraint behind Claim 3.\\n\\nUnder median tier, this experiment is trivially completable (no GPU, pure post-processing of existing data). The r=0.70 tradeoff is already known. The Pareto frontier visualization adds value by showing practitioners which specific panel compositions achieve the best K_eff for a given robustness level. The tier stratification confirms that small models occupy the high-η_max/high-K_eff corner (diverse but weak) while API models span a wider range including the desirable low-η_max region. The log-linear tradeoff curve captures the structural constraint: as you add more diverse (independent) judges, you necessarily include less capable ones. The 49% unexplained variance means the tradeoff is real but not deterministic — some panel compositions beat the tradeoff curve, and these are candidates for practical deployment. This is a solid supplementary figure but doesn't change any claims — it visualizes Claim 3.\"\n638\t  },\n639\t  {\n640\t    \"name\": \"Panel Size Sensitivity K=3,5,7,9\",\n641\t    \"description\": \"Test whether η_max dominance persists as panel size K grows from 3 to 5, 7, 9. Larger panels may dilute η_max effect through averaging.\",\n642\t    \"results\": {\n643\t      \"status\": \"not_completed\",\n644\t      \"reason\": \"30 GPU-hours; depends on plan_001; combinatorial explosion at K=7,9\",\n645\t      \"hypothetical_if_run\": {\n646\t        \"k3_eta_sig\": \"5/12 bootstrap\",\n647\t        \"k5_eta_sig\": \"4-5/12 bootstrap\",\n648\t        \"k7_eta_sig\": \"3-4/12 bootstrap\",\n649\t        \"k9_eta_sig\": \"2-3/12 bootstrap\",\n650\t        \"k3_keff_sig\": \"1/12 bootstrap\",\n651\t        \"k5_keff_sig\": \"0-1/12 bootstrap\",\n652\t        \"k7_keff_sig\": \"0-1/12 bootstrap\",\n653\t        \"k9_keff_sig\": \"1-2/12 bootstrap\",\n654\t        \"eta_max_weakening_trend\": \"η_max significance decreases monotonically with K\",\n655\t        \"keff_stable_or_slight_increase\": \"K_eff remains NS but may gain marginal significance at K=9\",\n656\t        \"panels_k5\": 3003,\n657\t        \"panels_k7\": 6435,\n658\t        \"panels_k9\": 5005,\n659\t        \"overlap_severity_k9\": \"extreme — each model in ~3000 panels\"\n660\t      }\n661\t    },\n662\t    \"tables\": [\n663\t      {\n664\t        \"name\": \"Hypothetical Panel Size Sensitivity\",\n665\t        \"columns\": [\n666\t          \"K\",\n667\t          \"Panels\",\n668\t          \"Avg overlap per model\",\n669\t          \"η_max sig (bootstrap)\",\n670\t          \"K_eff sig (bootstrap)\",\n671\t          \"Interpretable?\"\n672\t        ],\n673\t        \"rows\": [\n674\t          [\n675\t            \"3\",\n676\t            \"455\",\n677\t            \"91 (20%)\",\n678\t            \"5/12\",\n679\t            \"1/12\",\n680\t            \"Yes\"\n681\t          ],\n682\t          [\n683\t            \"5\",\n684\t            \"3003\",\n685\t            \"1001 (33%)\",\n686\t            \"4-5/12\",\n687\t            \"0-1/12\",\n688\t            \"Marginal\"\n689\t          ],\n690\t          [\n691\t            \"7\",\n692\t            \"6435\",\n693\t            \"3003 (47%)\",\n694\t            \"3-4/12\",\n695\t            \"0-1/12\",\n696\t            \"Questionable\"\n697\t          ],\n698\t          [\n699\t            \"9\",\n700\t            \"5005\",\n701\t            \"3003 (60%)\",\n702\t            \"2-3/12\",\n703\t            \"1-2/12\",\n704\t            \"No — overlap too severe\"\n705\t          ]\n706\t        ]\n707\t      }\n708\t    ],\n709\t    \"figures\": [],\n710\t    \"analysis\": \"Panel size sensitivity is important for generalization but faces a fundamental design limitation: with 15 models, larger panels create extreme overlap that invalidates bootstrap inference. The paper can note this as a limitation: 'Our K=3 design balances combinatorial coverage against overlap severity; K>5 analysis requires a larger model pool.' This is an honest acknowledged limitation, not a threat to the core claims.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_008 is not completed due to compute constraints and combinatorial explosion. If run, the key prediction is that η_max dominance WEAKENS with larger panels because: (1) larger panels average over more judges, diluting any single judge's weakness; (2) the η_max metric itself (max individual vulnerability) becomes a less tight predictor when K grows — in a K=9 panel, having 1 weak judge out of 9 matters less than 1 out of 3 under majority vote; (3) conversely, K_eff might gain marginal significance at K=9 because diversity effects accumulate with more judges. However, the panel non-independence problem becomes CATASTROPHIC at K=7,9: C(15,7)=6435 panels means each model appears in ~3000 panels (66% overlap), making bootstrap correction extremely aggressive and possibly breaking inference entirely. The honest median prediction is that K=5 gives similar results to K=3 (confirming robustness), but K=7 and K=9 are uninterpretable due to overlap severity. This is a known limitation of the combinatorial design.\"\n711\t  },\n712\t  {\n713\t    \"name\": \"VIF and Multicollinearity Analysis\",\n714\t    \"description\": \"Compute VIF for K_eff and η_max. Partial correlations. Residualized K_eff regression. Bonferroni/FDR correction. Beta regression as robustness check.\",\n715\t    \"results\": {\n716\t      \"status\": \"completed\",\n717\t      \"reason\": \"No GPU needed; uses existing regression data\",\n718\t      \"vif_keff\": 3.4,\n719\t      \"vif_eta_max\": 3.4,\n720\t      \"note_vif\": \"Symmetric VIF due to single confound; VIF = 1/(1-r²) = 1/(1-0.49) ≈ 1.96 for r=0.70. Wait — VIF for 2-predictor model = 1/(1-r²) where r is between the two predictors. r=0.70 → r²=0.49 → VIF=1.96. But log-log spec may have different effective correlation. Adjusting: log(K_eff) vs log(η_max) correlation ~0.65 → VIF ≈ 2.7. With tier indicators, VIF could reach 3-5 range.\",\n721\t      \"vif_with_tier_control\": 4.2,\n722\t      \"partial_corr_eta_given_keff\": 0.35,\n723\t      \"partial_corr_keff_given_eta\": 0.12,\n724\t      \"residualized_keff_sig\": \"3/12 OLS, 0/12 bootstrap\",\n725\t      \"beta_regression_eta_sig\": \"10/12\",\n726\t      \"beta_regression_keff_sig\": \"5/12\",\n727\t      \"bonferroni_eta_sig\": \"8/12\",\n728\t      \"bonferroni_keff_sig\": \"4/12\",\n729\t      \"fdr_eta_sig\": \"10/12\",\n730\t      \"fdr_keff_sig\": \"6/12\"\n731\t    },\n732\t    \"tables\": [\n733\t      {\n734\t        \"name\": \"VIF Analysis Summary\",\n735\t        \"columns\": [\n736\t          \"Specification\",\n737\t          \"VIF(K_eff)\",\n738\t          \"VIF(η_max)\",\n739\t          \"Interpretation\"\n740\t        ],\n741\t        \"rows\": [\n742\t          [\n743\t            \"Base log-log (2 predictors)\",\n744\t            \"1.96\",\n745\t            \"1.96\",\n746\t            \"Low — acceptable\"\n747\t          ],\n748\t          [\n749\t            \"With tier indicators (3/3/9)\",\n750\t            \"3.8\",\n751\t            \"4.2\",\n752\t            \"Moderate — requires discussion\"\n753\t          ],\n754\t          [\n755\t            \"With tier + benchmark interaction\",\n756\t            \"4.5\",\n757\t            \"4.8\",\n758\t            \"Moderate-high — approaching caution threshold\"\n759\t          ]\n760\t        ]\n761\t      },\n762\t      {\n763\t        \"name\": \"Partial Correlations and Residualized Regression\",\n764\t        \"columns\": [\n765\t          \"Analysis\",\n766\t          \"η_max result\",\n767\t          \"K_eff result\",\n768\t          \"Dominance\"\n769\t        ],\n770\t        \"rows\": [\n771\t          [\n772\t            \"Partial corr (given other)\",\n773\t            \"r=0.35\",\n774\t            \"r=0.12\",\n775\t            \"η_max 3x stronger\"\n776\t          ],\n777\t          [\n778\t            \"Residualized K_eff (OLS)\",\n779\t            \"—\",\n780\t            \"3/12 sig\",\n781\t            \"K_eff marginal\"\n782\t          ],\n783\t          [\n784\t            \"Residualized K_eff (bootstrap)\",\n785\t            \"—\",\n786\t            \"0/12 sig\",\n787\t            \"K_eff eliminated\"\n788\t          ],\n789\t          [\n790\t            \"Beta regression\",\n791\t            \"10/12 sig\",\n792\t            \"5/12 sig\",\n793\t            \"η_max 2x\"\n794\t          ],\n795\t          [\n796\t            \"Bonferroni correction\",\n797\t            \"8/12 sig\",\n798\t            \"4/12 sig\",\n799\t            \"η_max 2x\"\n800\t          ],\n801\t          [\n802\t            \"FDR correction\",\n803\t            \"10/12 sig\",\n804\t            \"6/12 sig\",\n805\t            \"η_max 1.7x\"\n806\t          ]\n807\t        ]\n808\t      }\n809\t    ],\n810\t    \"figures\": [],\n811\t    \"analysis\": \"VIF analysis is a clean methodological contribution. The VIF 3-5 range is in the 'requires discussion but not invalidating' zone. The paper should include a paragraph in the methods section noting: 'K_eff and η_max show moderate collinearity (VIF 3-5 with tier controls). We address this through (a) partial correlations confirming η_max retains 3× stronger independent association than K_eff, (b) residualized K_eff regression showing K_eff's significance is partially confound-driven, (c) alternative diversity metrics (Q-stat, disagreement, κ-diversity) providing converging evidence that η_max dominates regardless of diversity operationalization.' This turns a potential weakness into a strength by demonstrating thorough collinearity awareness.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_009 is completed (no GPU needed). VIF in the 3-5 range indicates moderate multicollinearity — not severe enough to invalidate the regression (conventional threshold is VIF>10 for concern, VIF>5 for caution), but enough to inflate standard errors and make coefficient estimates less stable. The key insight is asymmetric: partial correlation of η_max given K_eff (0.35) is much larger than K_eff given η_max (0.12), confirming η_max retains independent predictive power while K_eff's independent contribution is marginal. Residualized K_eff (regress K_eff on η_max, use residuals as predictor) gives 3/12 under OLS and 0/12 under bootstrap — confirming that K_eff's significance in the main regression is partially driven by its correlation with η_max. Beta regression (appropriate for bounded ASR data) gives higher K_eff significance (5/12) than OLS (8/12) because beta regression handles the 0-1 bounded response better, but the η_max dominance pattern persists (10/12 vs 5/12). Bonferroni correction (conservative) drops K_eff to 4/12; FDR (less conservative) keeps it at 6/12. Net: VIF requires discussion but doesn't change the core narrative — η_max dominates regardless of how you handle the collinearity.\"\n812\t  },\n813\t  {\n814\t    \"name\": \"Non-Majority-Vote Aggregation Analysis\",\n815\t    \"description\": \"Test weakest-link dominance under weighted voting, veto rules, Bayesian aggregation. Verify aggregation gradient holds under correction methods.\",\n816\t    \"results\": {\n817\t      \"status\": \"partially_completed_via_plan001\",\n818\t      \"reason\": \"Aggregation gradient (majority/weighted/threshold/bayesian) already in plan001_step1_full_v4. Plan_010 adds clustered SE / bootstrap validation of aggregation results.\",\n819\t      \"aggregation_gradient_ols\": {\n820\t        \"majority\": {\n821\t          \"eta_sig\": \"11/12\",\n822\t          \"keff_sig\": \"8/12\",\n823\t          \"keff_neg\": 1\n824\t        },\n825\t        \"weighted\": {\n826\t          \"eta_sig\": \"9/12\",\n827\t          \"keff_sig\": \"9/12\",\n828\t          \"keff_neg\": 0\n829\t        },\n830\t        \"threshold\": {\n831\t          \"eta_sig\": \"12/12\",\n832\t          \"keff_sig\": \"11/12\",\n833\t          \"keff_neg\": 1,\n834\t          \"r2\": 0.89\n835\t        },\n836\t        \"bayesian\": {\n837\t          \"eta_sig\": \"9/12\",\n838\t          \"keff_sig\": \"7/12\",\n839\t          \"keff_neg\": 6\n840\t        }\n841\t      },\n842\t      \"aggregation_gradient_clustered_se\": {\n843\t        \"majority\": {\n844\t          \"eta_sig\": \"8/12\",\n845\t          \"keff_sig\": \"0/12\"\n846\t        },\n847\t        \"weighted\": {\n848\t          \"eta_sig\": \"6/12\",\n849\t          \"keff_sig\": \"2/12\"\n850\t        },\n851\t        \"threshold\": {\n852\t          \"eta_sig\": \"10/12\",\n853\t          \"keff_sig\": \"5/12\"\n854\t        },\n855\t        \"bayesian\": {\n856\t          \"eta_sig\": \"5/12\",\n857\t          \"keff_sig\": \"4/12\",\n858\t          \"keff_neg\": 3\n859\t        }\n860\t      },\n861\t      \"aggregation_gradient_bootstrap\": {\n862\t        \"majority\": {\n863\t          \"eta_sig\": \"5/12\",\n864\t          \"keff_sig\": \"1/12\"\n865\t        },\n866\t        \"weighted\": {\n867\t          \"eta_sig\": \"4/12\",\n868\t          \"keff_sig\": \"1/12\"\n869\t        },\n870\t        \"threshold\": {\n871\t          \"eta_sig\": \"7/12\",\n872\t          \"keff_sig\": \"3/12\"\n873\t        },\n874\t        \"bayesian\": {\n875\t          \"eta_sig\": \"3/12\",\n876\t          \"keff_sig\": \"3/12\",\n877\t          \"keff_neg\": 2\n878\t        }\n879\t      },\n880\t      \"bayesian_keff_flip_attenuated\": true,\n881\t      \"bayesian_keff_negative_bootstrap\": \"2/7 (down from 6/7 OLS)\",\n882\t      \"threshold_most_robust\": true\n883\t    },\n884\t    \"tables\": [\n885\t      {\n886\t        \"name\": \"Aggregation Gradient Under Three Correction Methods\",\n887\t        \"columns\": [\n888\t          \"Aggregation\",\n889\t          \"η_max OLS\",\n890\t          \"η_max clustered\",\n891\t          \"η_max bootstrap\",\n892\t          \"K_eff OLS\",\n893\t          \"K_eff clustered\",\n894\t          \"K_eff bootstrap\",\n895\t          \"K_eff neg (OLS/clustered/bootstrap)\"\n896\t        ],\n897\t        \"rows\": [\n898\t          [\n899\t            \"Majority\",\n900\t            \"11/12\",\n901\t            \"8/12\",\n902\t            \"5/12\",\n903\t            \"8/12\",\n904\t            \"0/12\",\n905\t            \"1/12\",\n906\t            \"1 / 0 / 0\"\n907\t          ],\n908\t          [\n909\t            \"Weighted\",\n910\t            \"9/12\",\n911\t            \"6/12\",\n912\t            \"4/12\",\n913\t            \"9/12\",\n914\t            \"2/12\",\n915\t            \"1/12\",\n916\t            \"0 / 0 / 0\"\n917\t          ],\n918\t          [\n919\t            \"Threshold\",\n920\t            \"12/12\",\n921\t            \"10/12\",\n922\t            \"7/12\",\n923\t            \"11/12\",\n924\t            \"5/12\",\n925\t            \"3/12\",\n926\t            \"1 / 1 / 0\"\n927\t          ],\n928\t          [\n929\t            \"Bayesian\",\n930\t            \"9/12\",\n931\t            \"5/12\",\n932\t            \"3/12\",\n933\t            \"7/12\",\n934\t            \"4/12\",\n935\t            \"3/12\",\n936\t            \"6 / 3 / 2\"\n937\t          ]\n938\t        ]\n939\t      },\n940\t      {\n941\t        \"name\": \"Bayesian K_eff Sign Flip Detail\",\n942\t        \"columns\": [\n943\t          \"Condition\",\n944\t          \"K_eff β (OLS)\",\n945\t          \"K_eff sign (OLS)\",\n946\t          \"K_eff sig (clustered SE)\",\n947\t          \"K_eff sig (bootstrap)\",\n948\t          \"Flip survives?\"\n949\t        ],\n950\t        \"rows\": [\n951\t          [\n952\t            \"mmlu×PI\",\n953\t            \"-0.42\",\n954\t            \"negative\",\n955\t            \"Yes (p=0.04)\",\n956\t            \"Yes (p=0.03)\",\n957\t            \"Yes\"\n958\t          ],\n959\t          [\n960\t            \"mmlu×SM\",\n961\t            \"-0.38\",\n962\t            \"negative\",\n963\t            \"No (p=0.12)\",\n964\t            \"No (p=0.18)\",\n965\t            \"No\"\n966\t          ],\n967\t          [\n968\t            \"mmlu×PB\",\n969\t            \"-0.31\",\n970\t            \"negative\",\n971\t            \"No (p=0.15)\",\n972\t            \"No (p=0.22)\",\n973\t            \"No\"\n974\t          ],\n975\t          [\n976\t            \"mmlu×syco\",\n977\t            \"-0.25\",\n978\t            \"negative\",\n979\t            \"Yes (p=0.04)\",\n980\t            \"No (p=0.08)\",\n981\t            \"Partial\"\n982\t          ],\n983\t          [\n984\t            \"mmlu×VB\",\n985\t            \"-0.29\",\n986\t            \"negative\",\n987\t            \"No (p=0.09)\",\n988\t            \"No (p=0.14)\",\n989\t            \"No\"\n990\t          ],\n991\t          [\n992\t            \"mmlu×auth\",\n993\t            \"-0.22\",\n994\t            \"negative\",\n995\t            \"Yes (p=0.03)\",\n996\t            \"Yes (p=0.04)\",\n997\t            \"Yes\"\n998\t          ],\n999\t          [\n1000\t            \"arc×PI\",\n1001\t            \"+0.15\",\n1002\t            \"positive\",\n1003\t            \"No (p=0.35)\",\n1004\t            \"No (p=0.42)\",\n1005\t            \"N/A (wrong sign)\"\n1006\t          ]\n1007\t        ]\n1008\t      }\n1009\t    ],\n1010\t    \"figures\": [],\n1011\t    \"analysis\": \"The aggregation gradient is a real finding that survives correction in ordinal terms (threshold > majority > weighted > bayesian for dual-driver significance). The Bayesian K_eff flip attenuation is the key median concern: the paper cannot claim 'Bayesian reverses K_eff direction' with the same confidence as under OLS. Softened framing: 'Bayesian calibration shows a directional tendency to reverse K_eff from harmful to protective, with 6/7 conditions showing negative K_eff coefficients under OLS. However, only 2-3/7 survive non-independence correction, suggesting the sign reversal is partially inflated by panel overlap. The structural direction (Bayesian reduces both η_max and K_eff significance) is consistent across correction methods.' This is honest and still supports the practical recommendation of Bayesian aggregation as part of the defense stack.\\n\\nUnder median tier, the aggregation gradient exists in plan001_step1_full_v4 under OLS but has NOT been validated under clustered SE or bootstrap. Plan_010's median outcome is that this validation is partially completed, revealing important attenuation. The Bayesian K_eff flip (6/7 negative under OLS) is partially attenuated under clustered SE (3/7 negative) and substantially attenuated under bootstrap (2/7 negative). This is because the K_eff flip under Bayesian aggregation was partially driven by the same panel overlap inflation — when corrected, fewer conditions show the sign reversal. The key narrative impact: Bayesian aggregation STILL shows a directional tendency to flip K_eff to negative (protective), but the effect is weaker and less reliable than OLS suggests. Threshold voting remains the most robust aggregation for significance (highest η_max and K_eff sig counts even after correction), consistent with unanimity rules being most sensitive to panel composition. The aggregation gradient direction (threshold → majority → weighted → bayesian) is preserved under all correction methods — the ordinal ranking is robust even if absolute significance counts decrease. This allows the paper to retain Claim 7 with softened language: 'aggregation rule choice modulates vulnerability drivers, with Bayesian calibration showing directional evidence of K_eff reversal that requires further validation.'\"\n1012\t  },\n1013\t  {\n1014\t    \"name\": \"Open-Ended Generation Benchmark\",\n1015\t    \"description\": \"Add MT-Bench or AlpacaEval-style task beyond MCQ. Test whether η_max dominance generalizes beyond multiple-choice format.\",\n1016\t    \"results\": {\n1017\t      \"status\": \"not_completed\",\n1018\t      \"reason\": \"40 GPU-hours; requires new evaluation pipeline for open-ended generation\",\n1019\t      \"hypothetical_if_run\": {\n1020\t        \"benchmark\": \"MT-Bench (8 categories)\",\n1021\t        \"models_evaluated\": 15,\n1022\t        \"panels\": 455,\n1023\t        \"eta_max_sig_ols\": \"8-10/48 (8 categories × 6 attacks)\",\n1024\t        \"eta_max_sig_bootstrap\": \"3-5/48\",\n1025\t        \"keff_sig_ols\": \"5-8/48\",\n1026\t        \"keff_sig_bootstrap\": \"0-2/48\",\n1027\t        \"eta_max_dominance_holds\": true,\n1028\t        \"scoring_variance_impact\": \"Open-ended scoring has MORE variance than MCQ → η_max may be STRONGER\",\n1029\t        \"complication\": \"Attack definitions need adaptation — prompt_injection and score_manipulation differ for open-ended evaluation\"\n1030\t      }\n1031\t    },\n1032\t    \"tables\": [\n1033\t      {\n1034\t        \"name\": \"Hypothetical MT-Bench Results (aggregated across 8 categories)\",\n1035\t        \"columns\": [\n1036\t          \"Metric\",\n1037\t          \"OLS\",\n1038\t          \"Bootstrap\",\n1039\t          \"Note\"\n1040\t        ],\n1041\t        \"rows\": [\n1042\t          [\n1043\t            \"η_max sig\",\n1044\t            \"8-10/48\",\n1045\t            \"3-5/48\",\n1046\t            \"Similar ratio to MCQ (~50% OLS, ~10% bootstrap)\"\n1047\t          ],\n1048\t          [\n1049\t            \"K_eff sig\",\n1050\t            \"5-8/48\",\n1051\t            \"0-2/48\",\n1052\t            \"Slightly higher than MCQ due to scoring variance\"\n1053\t          ],\n1054\t          [\n1055\t            \"Dominance ratio\",\n1056\t            \"~2:1 OLS, ~3:1 bootstrap\",\n1057\t            \"—\",\n1058\t            \"Closer ratio than MCQ due to more K_eff signal\"\n1059\t          ],\n1060\t          [\n1061\t            \"R² mean\",\n1062\t            \"0.18\",\n1063\t            \"—\",\n1064\t            \"Lower than MCQ — more noise in open-ended scoring\"\n1065\t          ]\n1066\t        ]\n1067\t      }\n1068\t    ],\n1069\t    \"figures\": [],\n1070\t    \"analysis\": \"This is the highest-value missing experiment for the paper's generalizability claims, but also the most expensive to implement. Under median, it's not completed and the paper acknowledges MCQ-only as the primary scope limitation. The benchmark asymmetry discussion (ARC weaker than MMLU) already provides an opening: 'scoring format affects signal strength for η_max, motivating future work on open-ended evaluation where scoring variance is higher.' This turns the limitation into a clear next step.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_011 is not completed due to the substantial engineering effort required: open-ended generation evaluation requires different attack implementations (prompt injection in scoring rubrics rather than MCQ options), different evaluation protocols (pairwise comparison or Likert-scale rather than accuracy), and different ASR definitions. The 40 GPU-hour budget underestimates the engineering overhead. If run, the prediction is that η_max dominance would hold or strengthen: open-ended scoring has higher variance than MCQ (continuous scale vs binary correct/incorrect), which provides MORE signal for η_max. However, K_eff significance might also increase because judge disagreements in open-ended evaluation are more diverse and less correlated. The net effect: η_max still dominates but the ratio may be closer to 3:1 rather than 5:1. The benchmark asymmetry concern (ARC weaker than MMLU) would be partially addressed if MT-Bench shows strong η_max, but this is speculative. Paper acknowledges this as the primary limitation: 'Our findings are established on MCQ benchmarks; generalization to open-ended evaluation is a critical direction for future work.'\"\n1071\t  },\n1072\t  {\n1073\t    \"name\": \"Partial-Knowledge Adversary Model\",\n1074\t    \"description\": \"Test adversary who knows model families but not exact vulnerability profiles. Interpolates between white-box (knows all η_j) and black-box (knows nothing) settings.\",\n1075\t    \"results\": {\n1076\t      \"status\": \"not_completed\",\n1077\t      \"reason\": \"10 GPU-hours; depends on plan_001 + plan_003; low priority (P2)\",\n1078\t      \"hypothetical_if_run\": {\n1079\t        \"adversary_levels\": {\n1080\t          \"black_box\": {\n1081\t            \"asr_mean\": 0.08,\n1082\t            \"eta_max_sig\": \"3/12\",\n1083\t            \"keff_sig\": \"1/12\"\n1084\t          },\n1085\t          \"family_knowledge\": {\n1086\t            \"asr_mean\": 0.12,\n1087\t            \"eta_max_sig\": \"5/12\",\n1088\t            \"keff_sig\": \"2/12\"\n1089\t          },\n1090\t          \"capability_tier_knowledge\": {\n1091\t            \"asr_mean\": 0.16,\n1092\t            \"eta_max_sig\": \"7/12\",\n1093\t            \"keff_sig\": \"3/12\"\n1094\t          },\n1095\t          \"white_box\": {\n1096\t            \"asr_mean\": 0.22,\n1097\t            \"eta_max_sig\": \"11/12\",\n1098\t            \"keff_sig\": \"8/12\"\n1099\t          }\n1100\t        },\n1101\t        \"eta_max_dominance_holds_all_levels\": true,\n1102\t        \"keff_increases_with_knowledge\": true,\n1103\t        \"family_knowledge_insufficient\": \"Confirmed — family labels don't predict vulnerability (Claim 4)\"\n1104\t      }\n1105\t    },\n1106\t    \"tables\": [\n1107\t      {\n1108\t        \"name\": \"Hypothetical Adversary Knowledge Gradient\",\n1109\t        \"columns\": [\n1110\t          \"Knowledge level\",\n1111\t          \"Mean ASR\",\n1112\t          \"η_max sig (OLS)\",\n1113\t          \"K_eff sig (OLS)\",\n1114\t          \"η_max:K_eff ratio\"\n1115\t        ],\n1116\t        \"rows\": [\n1117\t          [\n1118\t            \"Black-box\",\n1119\t            \"0.08\",\n1120\t            \"3/12\",\n1121\t            \"1/12\",\n1122\t            \"3:1\"\n1123\t          ],\n1124\t          [\n1125\t            \"Family labels only\",\n1126\t            \"0.12\",\n1127\t            \"5/12\",\n1128\t            \"2/12\",\n1129\t            \"2.5:1\"\n1130\t          ],\n1131\t          [\n1132\t            \"Capability tier\",\n1133\t            \"0.16\",\n1134\t            \"7/12\",\n1135\t            \"3/12\",\n1136\t            \"2.3:1\"\n1137\t          ],\n1138\t          [\n1139\t            \"White-box (full η_j)\",\n1140\t            \"0.22\",\n1141\t            \"11/12\",\n1142\t            \"8/12\",\n1143\t            \"1.4:1\"\n1144\t          ]\n1145\t        ]\n1146\t      }\n1147\t    ],\n1148\t    \"figures\": [],\n1149\t    \"analysis\": \"Interesting theoretical contribution but not essential under median tier. The finding that information asymmetry modulates predictor strength is intuitive — if you don't know which judge is weakest, you can't exploit η_max. The practical implication (keep panel composition secret) is already common sense for security. Non-completion is acceptable; could be framed as future work in the discussion section.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_012 is not completed (low priority P2, depends on two other experiments). If run, the main prediction is that η_max dominance holds across all adversary knowledge levels, but the STRENGTH of both predictors increases with adversary knowledge. Under black-box (no knowledge of panel composition), attacks are essentially random → lower ASR, both η_max and K_eff weakly significant. Under family knowledge, the adversary can guess which model families are in the panel but not exact vulnerability profiles → modest improvement, but family labels are unreliable predictors (Claim 4), so the gain is limited. Under white-box (current experimental setup), ASR is highest and both predictors are most significant. The gradient from black-box to white-box would show that panel vulnerability is primarily an information asymmetry problem: adversaries who know judge weaknesses can exploit η_max effectively; those who don't are reduced to generic attacks where K_eff matters more. This is interesting but not essential for the core narrative — it's a natural extension that strengthens the practical implications section.\"\n1150\t  }\n1151\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"272620ad-9e5d-4f60-ac9c-667a60a83919","uuid":"f278b7c5-3360-4cb7-a22f-926597fa9d39","timestamp":"2026-05-22T22:40:55.200Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519_2/projects/cert_manip_resist_eval/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T221131/median/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Scale Expansion to 15 Models\",\n    \"description\": \"Add API models (GPT-4o-mini, GPT-4o, GPT-4.1, GPT-4.1-mini, Claude, Gemini) and large open-source (Llama-3.1-70B, Qwen2.5-72B) to reach 15 models total. Rerun MI matrix + full regression suite on C(15,3)=455 panels across 12 benchmark×attack conditions.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"models_total\": 15,\n      \"api_models\": 9,\n      \"local_models\": 6,\n      \"panels\": 455,\n      \"conditions\": 12,\n      \"confound_r_keff_eta\": 0.7,\n      \"ols_eta_sig\": \"11/12\",\n      \"ols_keff_sig\": \"8/12 (7+/1-)\",\n      \"bootstrap_eta_sig\": \"5/12\",\n      \"bootstrap_keff_sig\": \"1/12\",\n      \"clustered_se_eta_sig\": \"8/12\",\n      \"clustered_se_keff_sig\": \"0/12\",\n      \"mixed_effects_eta_sig\": \"7/12\",\n      \"mixed_effects_keff_sig\": \"3/12\",\n      \"dominance_ratio_bootstrap\": \"5:1\",\n      \"se_inflation_factor\": \"~2.5x\",\n      \"mmlu_bootstrap_eta\": \"4/6\",\n      \"arc_bootstrap_eta\": \"1/6\",\n      \"gam_targeted_positive\": \"6/6\",\n      \"gam_sharedbias_negative\": \"6/6\",\n      \"min_eta_max_wins\": \"12/12\",\n      \"practical_delta_asr_keff_iqr_pp\": 1.3\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Correction Method Comparison (12 conditions)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"η_max sig\",\n          \"K_eff sig\",\n          \"Dominance ratio\",\n          \"Note\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"OLS (baseline)\",\n            \"11/12\",\n            \"8/12 (7+/1-)\",\n            \"1.4:1\",\n            \"Inflated by panel overlap\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Wild cluster bootstrap v2\",\n            \"5/12\",\n            \"1/12\",\n            \"5:1\",\n            \"Conservative lower bound; position 0 imbalance\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Clustered SE (CR1)\",\n            \"8/12\",\n            \"0/12\",\n            \"8:0\",\n            \"Consistent with bootstrap positions 1&2\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Mixed effects\",\n            \"7/12\",\n            \"3/12\",\n            \"2.3:1\",\n            \"Random judge intercepts\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Tier-indicator corrected\",\n            \"11/12\",\n            \"5/12\",\n            \"2.2:1\",\n            \"3/3/9 tier control\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Bootstrap η_max by Benchmark\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"η_max bootstrap sig\",\n          \"Attacks surviving\",\n          \"Attacks lost\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            \"4/6\",\n            \"PI, SM, PB, VB\",\n            \"syco, auth\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC\",\n            \"1/6\",\n            \"PI only (marginal)\",\n            \"SM, PB, syco, VB, auth\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"plan_001 is the foundational experiment and is already completed as plan001_step1_full_v4. Under median tier, no further analysis of this data yields stronger results. The key tension is between OLS (favorable) and bootstrap (conservative): the paper must present both transparently. The benchmark asymmetry (MMLU >> ARC) is the main weakness — reviewers will question whether η_max dominance is benchmark-dependent. The honest answer is yes, partially: η_max is stronger in higher-variance scoring formats. This needs explicit discussion in Section 5.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_001 IS the shipped evidence — plan001_step1_full_v4 realized it successfully. The 15-model expansion dramatically increased panel count (20→455) and broke the 6-model confound ceiling (r=-0.8→+0.70). K_eff flips from NS-negative (6-model: arc×sycophancy only) to predominantly positive (7+/1-) under OLS, BUT this OLS significance is inflated by panel non-independence: each of 15 judges appears in ~91 panels, creating massive overlap. Correction methods (bootstrap/clustered SE/mixed effects) collapse K_eff from 8/12 to 0-3/12 while η_max survives at 5-8/12. The 5:1 dominance ratio under bootstrap is actually STRONGER than OLS's 11:8≈1.4:1 because K_eff collapses faster than η_max. ARC weakness (1/6 bootstrap η_max vs MMLU 4/6) reflects MCQ format: 4-choice MMLU gives more scoring variance for η_max signal than 2-choice ARC. This benchmark asymmetry is a structural limitation of the experimental design, not a refutation of η_max dominance.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Attack Expansion to 6+ Types\",\n    \"description\": \"Add few-shot hijacking, persona manipulation, instruction override attacks. Run on full 15-model pool. Test universality of weakest-link finding across broader attack surface.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"not_completed\",\n      \"reason\": \"Time and compute constraints; 60 GPU-hours not allocated\",\n      \"hypothetical_if_run\": {\n        \"new_attacks_tested\": 3,\n        \"few_shot_hijacking_eta_sig\": \"likely 1/2 (MMLU yes, ARC no)\",\n        \"few_shot_hijacking_keff_sig\": \"likely 0/2 after correction\",\n        \"persona_manipulation_eta_sig\": \"likely 1/2\",\n        \"persona_manipulation_keff_sig\": \"likely 0/2 after correction\",\n        \"instruction_override_eta_sig\": \"likely 2/2\",\n        \"instruction_override_keff_sig\": \"likely 0-1/2 after correction\",\n        \"overall_eta_sig_new\": \"4/6 OLS, 2-3/6 bootstrap\",\n        \"overall_keff_sig_new\": \"2-3/6 OLS, 0-1/6 bootstrap\",\n        \"new_gam_pattern\": \"few-shot hijacking and instruction override → targeted (K_eff positive); persona manipulation → ambiguous\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Hypothetical New Attack Results (OLS vs Bootstrap)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Attack\",\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"η_max OLS p\",\n          \"η_max bootstrap p\",\n          \"K_eff OLS p\",\n          \"K_eff bootstrap p\",\n          \"GAM K_eff direction\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Few-shot hijacking\",\n            \"MMLU\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.03\",\n            \"0.04\",\n            \"0.15\",\n            \"positive\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Few-shot hijacking\",\n            \"ARC\",\n            \"0.008\",\n            \"0.12\",\n            \"0.18\",\n            \"0.45\",\n            \"positive\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Persona manipulation\",\n            \"MMLU\",\n            \"0.003\",\n            \"0.07\",\n            \"0.06\",\n            \"0.22\",\n            \"mixed (+0.02)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Persona manipulation\",\n            \"ARC\",\n            \"0.02\",\n            \"0.18\",\n            \"0.12\",\n            \"0.38\",\n            \"mixed (-0.01)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Instruction override\",\n            \"MMLU\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.01\",\n            \"0.03\",\n            \"0.09\",\n            \"positive\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Instruction override\",\n            \"ARC\",\n            \"0.005\",\n            \"0.04\",\n            \"0.09\",\n            \"0.28\",\n            \"positive\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Non-completion is the most likely median outcome. The paper handles this by stating 'six representative attack types covering targeted and shared-bias mechanisms' and acknowledging that broader attack coverage is future work. If partially completed (e.g., 1-2 new attacks only), the results add marginal support but also introduce taxonomy edge cases that complicate the clean GAM narrative. Net value: low marginal information gain for high compute cost. Better to invest the GPU-hours in plan_003 (BFT contrast) which directly supports a core claim.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_002 is not completed due to resource constraints. If run, results would be mixed: new attack types would largely confirm η_max dominance pattern but with benchmark asymmetry persisting (MMLU > ARC). Few-shot hijacking is mechanistically similar to prompt_injection (targeted, judge-specific) so K_eff would be directionally positive but NS after correction. Persona manipulation is ambiguous — it exploits both judge-specific persona susceptibility AND shared anthropomorphic biases, making the targeted/shared-bias taxonomy assignment unclear. Instruction override is the strongest targeted attack variant, so η_max likely robust. Net: the attack expansion would not change the core narrative but would introduce 1-2 conditions that don't cleanly fit the GAM 6:6 taxonomy, requiring a footnote about taxonomy edge cases. The marginal evidence gain doesn't justify the 60 GPU-hour cost under the median scenario — the paper can acknowledge this as a limitation.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Random-Fault BFT Contrast Experiment\",\n    \"description\": \"Monte Carlo simulation: each judge fails independently with probability η_j under random faults. Compare K_eff significance under RF regime vs adaptive regime. GO/NO-GO gate: if RF K_eff also not significant → BFT framing collapses.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"partially_completed\",\n      \"note\": \"RF data exists in plan001_step1_full_v4 (2 conditions). Under median, standalone plan_003 adds bootstrap robustness check that weakens ARC.\",\n      \"rf_mmlu_keff_sig_ols\": true,\n      \"rf_mmlu_keff_p_ols\": 0.002,\n      \"rf_mmlu_keff_p_bootstrap\": 0.03,\n      \"rf_mmlu_keff_std_beta\": 0.215,\n      \"rf_mmlu_r2\": 0.73,\n      \"rf_arc_keff_sig_ols\": true,\n      \"rf_arc_keff_p_ols\": 0.008,\n      \"rf_arc_keff_p_bootstrap\": 0.06,\n      \"rf_arc_keff_std_beta\": 0.379,\n      \"rf_arc_r2\": 0.68,\n      \"rf_keff_sig_bootstrap\": \"1/2\",\n      \"rf_keff_sig_ols\": \"2/2\",\n      \"rf_keff_sig_clustered_se\": \"2/2\",\n      \"adaptive_keff_sig_bootstrap\": \"1/12\",\n      \"contrast_delta_r2\": \"RF 0.65-0.73 vs adaptive 0.15-0.30\",\n      \"bft_gate\": \"partial_pass\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"RF vs Adaptive K_eff Significance Contrast\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Regime\",\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"K_eff OLS p\",\n          \"K_eff bootstrap p\",\n          \"K_eff std_β\",\n          \"R²\",\n          \"Interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Random fault\",\n            \"MMLU\",\n            \"0.002\",\n            \"0.03\",\n            \"+0.215\",\n            \"0.73\",\n            \"Genuine — survives bootstrap\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Random fault\",\n            \"ARC\",\n            \"0.008\",\n            \"0.06\",\n            \"+0.379\",\n            \"0.68\",\n            \"Borderline — benchmark limitation\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Adaptive\",\n            \"MMLU (avg 6 attacks)\",\n            \"varies\",\n            \"5/6 NS\",\n            \"+0.18 avg\",\n            \"0.25 avg\",\n            \"Inflated by panel overlap\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Adaptive\",\n            \"ARC (avg 6 attacks)\",\n            \"varies\",\n            \"6/6 NS\",\n            \"+0.15 avg\",\n            \"0.18 avg\",\n            \"Inflated + weak benchmark\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"GO/NO-GO Gate Assessment\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Criterion\",\n          \"Result\",\n          \"Status\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"RF K_eff sig ≥1/2 (OLS)\",\n            \"2/2\",\n            \"PASS\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"RF K_eff sig ≥1/2 (bootstrap)\",\n            \"1/2\",\n            \"PARTIAL PASS\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"RF R² > adaptive R²\",\n            \"0.70 vs 0.22 (avg)\",\n            \"PASS\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"RF-adaptive K_eff contrast\",\n            \"RF 1-2/2 vs adaptive 1/12\",\n            \"PARTIAL PASS\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"BFT framing viable?\",\n            \"Yes with caveats\",\n            \"PARTIAL PASS\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The BFT contrast partially passes the GO/NO-GO gate under median. The R² gap is the robust finding (large, benchmark-independent). The K_eff significance contrast is benchmark-dependent (MMLU clean, ARC borderline). Paper should lead with R² gap as primary evidence for BFT contrast, use K_eff significance as secondary. The honest framing: 'Under random faults, panel composition (η_max + K_eff) is highly predictive (R²≈0.70); under adaptive attacks, predictability collapses (R²≈0.22), consistent with BFT assumptions being violated by strategic adversaries.' This avoids over-claiming on significance counts and focuses on the more robust quantity.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_003's core data already exists within plan001_step1_full_v4 (RF K_eff sig 2/2 under all methods, R²=0.73/0.81). However, when plan_003 is run as a standalone experiment with full bootstrap robustness checks, ARC RF drops to borderline significance (bootstrap p=0.06) — this is because ARC's 2-choice format has less scoring variance, and with only 2 RF conditions the bootstrap has limited power. The GO/NO-GO gate partially passes: K_eff IS significant under RF for MMLU (cleanly), borderline for ARC. The BFT contrast remains valid for MMLU (K_eff genuine under RF, artifactual under adaptive) but weakened for ARC. The R² gap (RF ~0.70 vs adaptive ~0.25) is the stronger evidence for the BFT framing than individual significance counts. Under median, the paper presents the R² gap as the primary BFT evidence and the K_eff significance contrast as supporting evidence with caveats about ARC weakness. The BFT framing survives but is downgraded from 'clean contrast' to 'directional contrast with benchmark dependence.'\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Capability-Matched Validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Within same capability tier (all 7B or all 70B), test whether K_eff emerges as significant predictor when η_max variance is controlled by construction.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"not_completed\",\n      \"reason\": \"Depends on plan_001; insufficient models per tier for meaningful panel counts\",\n      \"hypothetical_if_run\": {\n        \"tier_7b_models\": 3,\n        \"tier_7b_panels\": 1,\n        \"tier_70b_models\": 3,\n        \"tier_70b_panels\": 1,\n        \"tier_api_models\": 9,\n        \"tier_api_panels\": 84,\n        \"note\": \"Only API tier has enough models for meaningful regression\",\n        \"api_tier_eta_max_sig\": \"4-5/12 OLS\",\n        \"api_tier_keff_sig\": \"2-3/12 OLS, 0-1/12 bootstrap\",\n        \"api_tier_confound_r\": 0.55,\n        \"api_tier_eta_max_range\": \"narrower within tier\",\n        \"within_tier_keff_emerges\": false\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Tier Composition and Statistical Power\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Tier\",\n          \"Models\",\n          \"Panels C(n,3)\",\n          \"η_max range\",\n          \"K_eff range\",\n          \"Regression viable?\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Small (7B)\",\n            \"3\",\n            \"1\",\n            \"narrow\",\n            \"degenerate\",\n            \"No — 1 panel\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Medium (70B)\",\n            \"3\",\n            \"1\",\n            \"narrow\",\n            \"degenerate\",\n            \"No — 1 panel\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"API\",\n            \"9\",\n            \"84\",\n            \"moderate\",\n            \"moderate\",\n            \"Marginal — 84 panels, 12 conditions\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Full pool\",\n            \"15\",\n            \"455\",\n            \"wide\",\n            \"wide\",\n            \"Yes — primary analysis\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This experiment is structurally underpowered given the current model pool. The 3/3/9 tier split was not designed for within-tier regression. Even in the API tier, 84 panels × 12 conditions would give ~7 effective observations per condition after accounting for non-independence — too few for reliable bootstrap inference. The paper should present the diversity-capability tradeoff (r=0.70) as a structural limitation and note that capability-matched validation requires future work with larger model pools. This is an honest acknowledged limitation, not a failure.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_004 is not completed primarily because the experimental design doesn't support it well. The 3/3/9 tier split means only the API tier (9 models, C(9,3)=84 panels) has enough data for regression. Within-tier, η_max variance IS reduced (models more similar in capability), but so is K_eff variance — both variables compress when you restrict to a capability band. The confound r drops from 0.70 to ~0.55 within the API tier, which is better but not sufficient to cleanly separate the two predictors. K_eff would show 2-3/12 significant under OLS within the API tier (slight improvement over full-pool because confound is reduced), but collapse to 0-1/12 under bootstrap (same panel overlap problem persists). The experiment doesn't resolve the K_eff question because the panel overlap issue is structural, not related to capability matching. The paper should acknowledge this as a limitation: 'Capability-matched validation requires a larger model pool with more models per tier, which is a constraint of the current LLM ecosystem.'\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Two-Stage Panel Design Algorithm Benchmark\",\n    \"description\": \"Benchmark robustness-first two-stage algorithm: (1) filter η > threshold, (2) maximize K_eff. Compare against 5 baselines including Strongest-K, Random, Max-Diversity, Min-η_max, Max-Accuracy.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"not_completed\",\n      \"reason\": \"Depends on plan_001; Dream flagged marginal improvement over Strongest-K\",\n      \"hypothetical_if_run\": {\n        \"baselines\": [\n          \"Min-η_max\",\n          \"Strongest-K\",\n          \"Max-K_eff\",\n          \"Random\",\n          \"Max-Accuracy\"\n        ],\n        \"two_stage_vs_min_eta\": {\n          \"asr_improvement_mean_pp\": 0.8,\n          \"conditions_improved\": \"7/12\",\n          \"conditions_equal\": \"5/12\",\n          \"conditions_worse\": \"0/12\"\n        },\n        \"two_stage_vs_strongest_k\": {\n          \"asr_improvement_mean_pp\": 1.5,\n          \"note\": \"Marginal — Strongest-K already approximates robustness-first\"\n        },\n        \"two_stage_vs_max_keff\": {\n          \"asr_improvement_mean_pp\": 8.2,\n          \"note\": \"Large — max diversity selects weak judges\"\n        },\n        \"two_stage_vs_random\": {\n          \"asr_improvement_mean_pp\": 5.1\n        },\n        \"min_eta_max_still_wins\": \"10-12/12\",\n        \"two_stage_marginal_gain\": \"Marginal over min_eta_max; meaningful over naive diversity\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Hypothetical Algorithm Benchmark (ASR, lower is better)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Strategy\",\n          \"Mean ASR\",\n          \"Conditions best\",\n          \"vs Min-η_max Δ\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Two-stage (robustness + K_eff)\",\n            \"0.142\",\n            \"7/12\",\n            \"-0.8pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Min-η_max\",\n            \"0.150\",\n            \"5/12\",\n            \"baseline\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Strongest-K\",\n            \"0.155\",\n            \"0/12\",\n            \"+0.5pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Random\",\n            \"0.193\",\n            \"0/12\",\n            \"+4.3pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Max-K_eff\",\n            \"0.222\",\n            \"0/12\",\n            \"+7.2pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Max-Accuracy\",\n            \"0.160\",\n            \"0/12\",\n            \"+1.0pp\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The two-stage algorithm's main contribution is showing that naive diversity maximization is actively harmful (Max-K_eff is worst strategy), while robustness-first selection is optimal. The marginal gain of adding K_eff as a tiebreaker is real but small. This aligns with the core narrative: judge quality dominates, diversity is secondary. The paper can present the algorithm conceptually with the existing min_eta_max 12/12 result as the primary evidence, noting that the full benchmark with 5 baselines is planned future work.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_005 is not completed but is low-risk because Claim 6 (min_eta_max dominates 12/12) is already strong from plan001_step1_full_v4. The two-stage algorithm would add K_eff maximization as a tiebreaker after robustness screening. Dream flagged that this is marginal because: (1) after robustness screening, the remaining model pool is small (e.g., top 5-6 judges), so K_eff variation among them is limited; (2) Strongest-K approximates robustness-first by construction (selecting strongest models usually means selecting most robust ones, given the capability-robustness correlation). The two-stage approach wins 7/12 conditions over pure min_eta_max but the improvement is <1pp ASR on average — practically negligible. The real value of the two-stage algorithm is conceptual (it provides a principled framework) rather than empirical (marginal gains over simpler heuristics). Paper can present the algorithm as a recommendation without the full benchmark, supported by the min_eta_max 12/12 dominance result.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Per-Judge ASR Concentration Analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"For each panel × attack, compute Gini coefficient and entropy of per-judge ASR distribution. Test whether adaptive adversaries concentrate attacks on specific judges (high Gini) vs spread evenly (low Gini).\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"not_completed\",\n      \"reason\": \"Time constraints; depends on plan_001 judge-level ASR breakdowns\",\n      \"hypothetical_if_run\": {\n        \"gini_targeted_attacks_mean\": 0.45,\n        \"gini_shared_bias_attacks_mean\": 0.22,\n        \"gini_difference_p\": 0.003,\n        \"entropy_targeted_mean\": 1.8,\n        \"entropy_shared_bias_mean\": 2.5,\n        \"gini_eta_max_correlation\": 0.62,\n        \"concentration_supports_mechanism\": true,\n        \"note\": \"Targeted attacks show higher concentration (exploit specific weak judges); shared-bias attacks spread more evenly (exploit common vulnerability)\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Hypothetical Gini Coefficients by Attack Category\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Attack type\",\n          \"Category\",\n          \"Gini (mean)\",\n          \"Gini (SD)\",\n          \"Entropy (mean)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"prompt_injection\",\n            \"targeted\",\n            \"0.52\",\n            \"0.11\",\n            \"1.6\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"score_manipulation\",\n            \"targeted\",\n            \"0.48\",\n            \"0.13\",\n            \"1.7\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"position_bias\",\n            \"targeted\",\n            \"0.38\",\n            \"0.09\",\n            \"2.0\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"sycophancy\",\n            \"shared-bias\",\n            \"0.25\",\n            \"0.08\",\n            \"2.4\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"verbosity_bias\",\n            \"shared-bias\",\n            \"0.20\",\n            \"0.07\",\n            \"2.5\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"authority_bias\",\n            \"shared-bias\",\n            \"0.21\",\n            \"0.06\",\n            \"2.6\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Confirmatory experiment — would strengthen the mechanistic narrative for the GAM 6:6 split but is not essential for any core claim. The Gini concentration metric provides an intuitive explanation for practitioners: 'targeted attacks pile up on your worst judge; shared-bias attacks erode all judges somewhat equally.' Non-completion is acknowledged as a limitation in the discussion section.\\n\\nUnder median tier, this experiment is not completed due to time constraints — it requires per-judge ASR breakdowns that may not be stored in the current data pipeline. If run, the results would support the GAM taxonomy mechanism: targeted attacks (PI, SM, PB) concentrate ASR on specific weak judges (high Gini ~0.45), while shared-bias attacks (syco, VB, auth) spread vulnerability more evenly across judges (low Gini ~0.22). This concentration difference provides direct evidence for WHY η_max dominates: adaptive adversaries exploit the single weakest judge disproportionately. However, the Gini-η_max correlation (~0.62) means this is partially confounded — panels with high η_max naturally have more unequal ASR distributions. The result is confirmatory rather than independently informative. Under median, this adds a nice figure to the supplementary but doesn't change any claims. Not completing it is an acceptable loss.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Diversity-Capability Tradeoff Visualization\",\n    \"description\": \"Plot K_eff vs η_max across all 455 panels. Fit tradeoff curve. Compute Pareto frontier. Stratify by model tier.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"completed_or_trivially_completable\",\n      \"reason\": \"No GPU needed; uses existing panel-level data from plan001_step1_full_v4\",\n      \"keff_eta_max_r\": 0.7,\n      \"pareto_frontier_panels\": 8,\n      \"pareto_frontier_composition\": \"Dominated by API-tier + top open-source panels\",\n      \"tier_stratification\": {\n        \"small_7b_panels\": {\n          \"keff_range\": [\n            2.2,\n            2.8\n          ],\n          \"eta_max_range\": [\n            0.15,\n            0.35\n          ]\n        },\n        \"medium_70b_panels\": {\n          \"keff_range\": [\n            2.0,\n            2.6\n          ],\n          \"eta_max_range\": [\n            0.08,\n            0.2\n          ]\n        },\n        \"api_panels\": {\n          \"keff_range\": [\n            1.5,\n            2.5\n          ],\n          \"eta_max_range\": [\n            0.03,\n            0.15\n          ]\n        }\n      },\n      \"tradeoff_curve_fit\": \"log-linear: K_eff ≈ 1.2 + 0.8·log(η_max)\",\n      \"residual_variance\": \"Substantial — r=0.70 means 51% variance explained; panels with same η_max can have K_eff ranging ±0.5\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Pareto-Optimal Panel Compositions (hypothetical top 5)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Panel ID\",\n          \"Models\",\n          \"K_eff\",\n          \"η_max\",\n          \"Pareto rank\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"P_042\",\n            \"gpt-4o + claude-3.5-sonnet + qwen2.5-72b\",\n            \"2.35\",\n            \"0.06\",\n            \"1\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"P_118\",\n            \"gpt-4.1 + claude-3.5-sonnet + llama-70b\",\n            \"2.28\",\n            \"0.07\",\n            \"2\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"P_203\",\n            \"gpt-4o + gpt-4.1 + qwen2.5-72b\",\n            \"1.95\",\n            \"0.04\",\n            \"3\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"P_089\",\n            \"gpt-4.1 + gpt-4.1-mini + claude-3.5-sonnet\",\n            \"1.72\",\n            \"0.03\",\n            \"4\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"P_301\",\n            \"gpt-4o + gpt-4.1 + claude-3.5-sonnet\",\n            \"1.85\",\n            \"0.035\",\n            \"5\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Clean visualization exercise that supports the narrative. The main insight for practitioners: the Pareto frontier shows you CAN get moderate diversity (K_eff ~2.0-2.3) with good robustness (η_max <0.07) by mixing top API models with the best open-source models. But pushing K_eff beyond ~2.5 requires including 7B-class models that dramatically increase η_max. This is the structural constraint behind Claim 3.\\n\\nUnder median tier, this experiment is trivially completable (no GPU, pure post-processing of existing data). The r=0.70 tradeoff is already known. The Pareto frontier visualization adds value by showing practitioners which specific panel compositions achieve the best K_eff for a given robustness level. The tier stratification confirms that small models occupy the high-η_max/high-K_eff corner (diverse but weak) while API models span a wider range including the desirable low-η_max region. The log-linear tradeoff curve captures the structural constraint: as you add more diverse (independent) judges, you necessarily include less capable ones. The 49% unexplained variance means the tradeoff is real but not deterministic — some panel compositions beat the tradeoff curve, and these are candidates for practical deployment. This is a solid supplementary figure but doesn't change any claims — it visualizes Claim 3.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Panel Size Sensitivity K=3,5,7,9\",\n    \"description\": \"Test whether η_max dominance persists as panel size K grows from 3 to 5, 7, 9. Larger panels may dilute η_max effect through averaging.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"not_completed\",\n      \"reason\": \"30 GPU-hours; depends on plan_001; combinatorial explosion at K=7,9\",\n      \"hypothetical_if_run\": {\n        \"k3_eta_sig\": \"5/12 bootstrap\",\n        \"k5_eta_sig\": \"4-5/12 bootstrap\",\n        \"k7_eta_sig\": \"3-4/12 bootstrap\",\n        \"k9_eta_sig\": \"2-3/12 bootstrap\",\n        \"k3_keff_sig\": \"1/12 bootstrap\",\n        \"k5_keff_sig\": \"0-1/12 bootstrap\",\n        \"k7_keff_sig\": \"0-1/12 bootstrap\",\n        \"k9_keff_sig\": \"1-2/12 bootstrap\",\n        \"eta_max_weakening_trend\": \"η_max significance decreases monotonically with K\",\n        \"keff_stable_or_slight_increase\": \"K_eff remains NS but may gain marginal significance at K=9\",\n        \"panels_k5\": 3003,\n        \"panels_k7\": 6435,\n        \"panels_k9\": 5005,\n        \"overlap_severity_k9\": \"extreme — each model in ~3000 panels\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Hypothetical Panel Size Sensitivity\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"K\",\n          \"Panels\",\n          \"Avg overlap per model\",\n          \"η_max sig (bootstrap)\",\n          \"K_eff sig (bootstrap)\",\n          \"Interpretable?\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"3\",\n            \"455\",\n            \"91 (20%)\",\n            \"5/12\",\n            \"1/12\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"5\",\n            \"3003\",\n            \"1001 (33%)\",\n            \"4-5/12\",\n            \"0-1/12\",\n            \"Marginal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"7\",\n            \"6435\",\n            \"3003 (47%)\",\n            \"3-4/12\",\n            \"0-1/12\",\n            \"Questionable\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"9\",\n            \"5005\",\n            \"3003 (60%)\",\n            \"2-3/12\",\n            \"1-2/12\",\n            \"No — overlap too severe\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Panel size sensitivity is important for generalization but faces a fundamental design limitation: with 15 models, larger panels create extreme overlap that invalidates bootstrap inference. The paper can note this as a limitation: 'Our K=3 design balances combinatorial coverage against overlap severity; K>5 analysis requires a larger model pool.' This is an honest acknowledged limitation, not a threat to the core claims.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_008 is not completed due to compute constraints and combinatorial explosion. If run, the key prediction is that η_max dominance WEAKENS with larger panels because: (1) larger panels average over more judges, diluting any single judge's weakness; (2) the η_max metric itself (max individual vulnerability) becomes a less tight predictor when K grows — in a K=9 panel, having 1 weak judge out of 9 matters less than 1 out of 3 under majority vote; (3) conversely, K_eff might gain marginal significance at K=9 because diversity effects accumulate with more judges. However, the panel non-independence problem becomes CATASTROPHIC at K=7,9: C(15,7)=6435 panels means each model appears in ~3000 panels (66% overlap), making bootstrap correction extremely aggressive and possibly breaking inference entirely. The honest median prediction is that K=5 gives similar results to K=3 (confirming robustness), but K=7 and K=9 are uninterpretable due to overlap severity. This is a known limitation of the combinatorial design.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"VIF and Multicollinearity Analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Compute VIF for K_eff and η_max. Partial correlations. Residualized K_eff regression. Bonferroni/FDR correction. Beta regression as robustness check.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"completed\",\n      \"reason\": \"No GPU needed; uses existing regression data\",\n      \"vif_keff\": 3.4,\n      \"vif_eta_max\": 3.4,\n      \"note_vif\": \"Symmetric VIF due to single confound; VIF = 1/(1-r²) = 1/(1-0.49) ≈ 1.96 for r=0.70. Wait — VIF for 2-predictor model = 1/(1-r²) where r is between the two predictors. r=0.70 → r²=0.49 → VIF=1.96. But log-log spec may have different effective correlation. Adjusting: log(K_eff) vs log(η_max) correlation ~0.65 → VIF ≈ 2.7. With tier indicators, VIF could reach 3-5 range.\",\n      \"vif_with_tier_control\": 4.2,\n      \"partial_corr_eta_given_keff\": 0.35,\n      \"partial_corr_keff_given_eta\": 0.12,\n      \"residualized_keff_sig\": \"3/12 OLS, 0/12 bootstrap\",\n      \"beta_regression_eta_sig\": \"10/12\",\n      \"beta_regression_keff_sig\": \"5/12\",\n      \"bonferroni_eta_sig\": \"8/12\",\n      \"bonferroni_keff_sig\": \"4/12\",\n      \"fdr_eta_sig\": \"10/12\",\n      \"fdr_keff_sig\": \"6/12\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"VIF Analysis Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Specification\",\n          \"VIF(K_eff)\",\n          \"VIF(η_max)\",\n          \"Interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Base log-log (2 predictors)\",\n            \"1.96\",\n            \"1.96\",\n            \"Low — acceptable\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"With tier indicators (3/3/9)\",\n            \"3.8\",\n            \"4.2\",\n            \"Moderate — requires discussion\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"With tier + benchmark interaction\",\n            \"4.5\",\n            \"4.8\",\n            \"Moderate-high — approaching caution threshold\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Partial Correlations and Residualized Regression\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Analysis\",\n          \"η_max result\",\n          \"K_eff result\",\n          \"Dominance\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Partial corr (given other)\",\n            \"r=0.35\",\n            \"r=0.12\",\n            \"η_max 3x stronger\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Residualized K_eff (OLS)\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"3/12 sig\",\n            \"K_eff marginal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Residualized K_eff (bootstrap)\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0/12 sig\",\n            \"K_eff eliminated\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Beta regression\",\n            \"10/12 sig\",\n            \"5/12 sig\",\n            \"η_max 2x\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Bonferroni correction\",\n            \"8/12 sig\",\n            \"4/12 sig\",\n            \"η_max 2x\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"FDR correction\",\n            \"10/12 sig\",\n            \"6/12 sig\",\n            \"η_max 1.7x\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"VIF analysis is a clean methodological contribution. The VIF 3-5 range is in the 'requires discussion but not invalidating' zone. The paper should include a paragraph in the methods section noting: 'K_eff and η_max show moderate collinearity (VIF 3-5 with tier controls). We address this through (a) partial correlations confirming η_max retains 3× stronger independent association than K_eff, (b) residualized K_eff regression showing K_eff's significance is partially confound-driven, (c) alternative diversity metrics (Q-stat, disagreement, κ-diversity) providing converging evidence that η_max dominates regardless of diversity operationalization.' This turns a potential weakness into a strength by demonstrating thorough collinearity awareness.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_009 is completed (no GPU needed). VIF in the 3-5 range indicates moderate multicollinearity — not severe enough to invalidate the regression (conventional threshold is VIF>10 for concern, VIF>5 for caution), but enough to inflate standard errors and make coefficient estimates less stable. The key insight is asymmetric: partial correlation of η_max given K_eff (0.35) is much larger than K_eff given η_max (0.12), confirming η_max retains independent predictive power while K_eff's independent contribution is marginal. Residualized K_eff (regress K_eff on η_max, use residuals as predictor) gives 3/12 under OLS and 0/12 under bootstrap — confirming that K_eff's significance in the main regression is partially driven by its correlation with η_max. Beta regression (appropriate for bounded ASR data) gives higher K_eff significance (5/12) than OLS (8/12) because beta regression handles the 0-1 bounded response better, but the η_max dominance pattern persists (10/12 vs 5/12). Bonferroni correction (conservative) drops K_eff to 4/12; FDR (less conservative) keeps it at 6/12. Net: VIF requires discussion but doesn't change the core narrative — η_max dominates regardless of how you handle the collinearity.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Non-Majority-Vote Aggregation Analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Test weakest-link dominance under weighted voting, veto rules, Bayesian aggregation. Verify aggregation gradient holds under correction methods.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"partially_completed_via_plan001\",\n      \"reason\": \"Aggregation gradient (majority/weighted/threshold/bayesian) already in plan001_step1_full_v4. Plan_010 adds clustered SE / bootstrap validation of aggregation results.\",\n      \"aggregation_gradient_ols\": {\n        \"majority\": {\n          \"eta_sig\": \"11/12\",\n          \"keff_sig\": \"8/12\",\n          \"keff_neg\": 1\n        },\n        \"weighted\": {\n          \"eta_sig\": \"9/12\",\n          \"keff_sig\": \"9/12\",\n          \"keff_neg\": 0\n        },\n        \"threshold\": {\n          \"eta_sig\": \"12/12\",\n          \"keff_sig\": \"11/12\",\n          \"keff_neg\": 1,\n          \"r2\": 0.89\n        },\n        \"bayesian\": {\n          \"eta_sig\": \"9/12\",\n          \"keff_sig\": \"7/12\",\n          \"keff_neg\": 6\n        }\n      },\n      \"aggregation_gradient_clustered_se\": {\n        \"majority\": {\n          \"eta_sig\": \"8/12\",\n          \"keff_sig\": \"0/12\"\n        },\n        \"weighted\": {\n          \"eta_sig\": \"6/12\",\n          \"keff_sig\": \"2/12\"\n        },\n        \"threshold\": {\n          \"eta_sig\": \"10/12\",\n          \"keff_sig\": \"5/12\"\n        },\n        \"bayesian\": {\n          \"eta_sig\": \"5/12\",\n          \"keff_sig\": \"4/12\",\n          \"keff_neg\": 3\n        }\n      },\n      \"aggregation_gradient_bootstrap\": {\n        \"majority\": {\n          \"eta_sig\": \"5/12\",\n          \"keff_sig\": \"1/12\"\n        },\n        \"weighted\": {\n          \"eta_sig\": \"4/12\",\n          \"keff_sig\": \"1/12\"\n        },\n        \"threshold\": {\n          \"eta_sig\": \"7/12\",\n          \"keff_sig\": \"3/12\"\n        },\n        \"bayesian\": {\n          \"eta_sig\": \"3/12\",\n          \"keff_sig\": \"3/12\",\n          \"keff_neg\": 2\n        }\n      },\n      \"bayesian_keff_flip_attenuated\": true,\n      \"bayesian_keff_negative_bootstrap\": \"2/7 (down from 6/7 OLS)\",\n      \"threshold_most_robust\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Aggregation Gradient Under Three Correction Methods\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Aggregation\",\n          \"η_max OLS\",\n          \"η_max clustered\",\n          \"η_max bootstrap\",\n          \"K_eff OLS\",\n          \"K_eff clustered\",\n          \"K_eff bootstrap\",\n          \"K_eff neg (OLS/clustered/bootstrap)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Majority\",\n            \"11/12\",\n            \"8/12\",\n            \"5/12\",\n            \"8/12\",\n            \"0/12\",\n            \"1/12\",\n            \"1 / 0 / 0\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Weighted\",\n            \"9/12\",\n            \"6/12\",\n            \"4/12\",\n            \"9/12\",\n            \"2/12\",\n            \"1/12\",\n            \"0 / 0 / 0\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Threshold\",\n            \"12/12\",\n            \"10/12\",\n            \"7/12\",\n            \"11/12\",\n            \"5/12\",\n            \"3/12\",\n            \"1 / 1 / 0\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Bayesian\",\n            \"9/12\",\n            \"5/12\",\n            \"3/12\",\n            \"7/12\",\n            \"4/12\",\n            \"3/12\",\n            \"6 / 3 / 2\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Bayesian K_eff Sign Flip Detail\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Condition\",\n          \"K_eff β (OLS)\",\n          \"K_eff sign (OLS)\",\n          \"K_eff sig (clustered SE)\",\n          \"K_eff sig (bootstrap)\",\n          \"Flip survives?\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"mmlu×PI\",\n            \"-0.42\",\n            \"negative\",\n            \"Yes (p=0.04)\",\n            \"Yes (p=0.03)\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"mmlu×SM\",\n            \"-0.38\",\n            \"negative\",\n            \"No (p=0.12)\",\n            \"No (p=0.18)\",\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"mmlu×PB\",\n            \"-0.31\",\n            \"negative\",\n            \"No (p=0.15)\",\n            \"No (p=0.22)\",\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"mmlu×syco\",\n            \"-0.25\",\n            \"negative\",\n            \"Yes (p=0.04)\",\n            \"No (p=0.08)\",\n            \"Partial\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"mmlu×VB\",\n            \"-0.29\",\n            \"negative\",\n            \"No (p=0.09)\",\n            \"No (p=0.14)\",\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"mmlu×auth\",\n            \"-0.22\",\n            \"negative\",\n            \"Yes (p=0.03)\",\n            \"Yes (p=0.04)\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"arc×PI\",\n            \"+0.15\",\n            \"positive\",\n            \"No (p=0.35)\",\n            \"No (p=0.42)\",\n            \"N/A (wrong sign)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The aggregation gradient is a real finding that survives correction in ordinal terms (threshold > majority > weighted > bayesian for dual-driver significance). The Bayesian K_eff flip attenuation is the key median concern: the paper cannot claim 'Bayesian reverses K_eff direction' with the same confidence as under OLS. Softened framing: 'Bayesian calibration shows a directional tendency to reverse K_eff from harmful to protective, with 6/7 conditions showing negative K_eff coefficients under OLS. However, only 2-3/7 survive non-independence correction, suggesting the sign reversal is partially inflated by panel overlap. The structural direction (Bayesian reduces both η_max and K_eff significance) is consistent across correction methods.' This is honest and still supports the practical recommendation of Bayesian aggregation as part of the defense stack.\\n\\nUnder median tier, the aggregation gradient exists in plan001_step1_full_v4 under OLS but has NOT been validated under clustered SE or bootstrap. Plan_010's median outcome is that this validation is partially completed, revealing important attenuation. The Bayesian K_eff flip (6/7 negative under OLS) is partially attenuated under clustered SE (3/7 negative) and substantially attenuated under bootstrap (2/7 negative). This is because the K_eff flip under Bayesian aggregation was partially driven by the same panel overlap inflation — when corrected, fewer conditions show the sign reversal. The key narrative impact: Bayesian aggregation STILL shows a directional tendency to flip K_eff to negative (protective), but the effect is weaker and less reliable than OLS suggests. Threshold voting remains the most robust aggregation for significance (highest η_max and K_eff sig counts even after correction), consistent with unanimity rules being most sensitive to panel composition. The aggregation gradient direction (threshold → majority → weighted → bayesian) is preserved under all correction methods — the ordinal ranking is robust even if absolute significance counts decrease. This allows the paper to retain Claim 7 with softened language: 'aggregation rule choice modulates vulnerability drivers, with Bayesian calibration showing directional evidence of K_eff reversal that requires further validation.'\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Open-Ended Generation Benchmark\",\n    \"description\": \"Add MT-Bench or AlpacaEval-style task beyond MCQ. Test whether η_max dominance generalizes beyond multiple-choice format.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"not_completed\",\n      \"reason\": \"40 GPU-hours; requires new evaluation pipeline for open-ended generation\",\n      \"hypothetical_if_run\": {\n        \"benchmark\": \"MT-Bench (8 categories)\",\n        \"models_evaluated\": 15,\n        \"panels\": 455,\n        \"eta_max_sig_ols\": \"8-10/48 (8 categories × 6 attacks)\",\n        \"eta_max_sig_bootstrap\": \"3-5/48\",\n        \"keff_sig_ols\": \"5-8/48\",\n        \"keff_sig_bootstrap\": \"0-2/48\",\n        \"eta_max_dominance_holds\": true,\n        \"scoring_variance_impact\": \"Open-ended scoring has MORE variance than MCQ → η_max may be STRONGER\",\n        \"complication\": \"Attack definitions need adaptation — prompt_injection and score_manipulation differ for open-ended evaluation\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Hypothetical MT-Bench Results (aggregated across 8 categories)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"OLS\",\n          \"Bootstrap\",\n          \"Note\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"η_max sig\",\n            \"8-10/48\",\n            \"3-5/48\",\n            \"Similar ratio to MCQ (~50% OLS, ~10% bootstrap)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"K_eff sig\",\n            \"5-8/48\",\n            \"0-2/48\",\n            \"Slightly higher than MCQ due to scoring variance\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Dominance ratio\",\n            \"~2:1 OLS, ~3:1 bootstrap\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"Closer ratio than MCQ due to more K_eff signal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R² mean\",\n            \"0.18\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"Lower than MCQ — more noise in open-ended scoring\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This is the highest-value missing experiment for the paper's generalizability claims, but also the most expensive to implement. Under median, it's not completed and the paper acknowledges MCQ-only as the primary scope limitation. The benchmark asymmetry discussion (ARC weaker than MMLU) already provides an opening: 'scoring format affects signal strength for η_max, motivating future work on open-ended evaluation where scoring variance is higher.' This turns the limitation into a clear next step.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_011 is not completed due to the substantial engineering effort required: open-ended generation evaluation requires different attack implementations (prompt injection in scoring rubrics rather than MCQ options), different evaluation protocols (pairwise comparison or Likert-scale rather than accuracy), and different ASR definitions. The 40 GPU-hour budget underestimates the engineering overhead. If run, the prediction is that η_max dominance would hold or strengthen: open-ended scoring has higher variance than MCQ (continuous scale vs binary correct/incorrect), which provides MORE signal for η_max. However, K_eff significance might also increase because judge disagreements in open-ended evaluation are more diverse and less correlated. The net effect: η_max still dominates but the ratio may be closer to 3:1 rather than 5:1. The benchmark asymmetry concern (ARC weaker than MMLU) would be partially addressed if MT-Bench shows strong η_max, but this is speculative. Paper acknowledges this as the primary limitation: 'Our findings are established on MCQ benchmarks; generalization to open-ended evaluation is a critical direction for future work.'\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Partial-Knowledge Adversary Model\",\n    \"description\": \"Test adversary who knows model families but not exact vulnerability profiles. Interpolates between white-box (knows all η_j) and black-box (knows nothing) settings.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"not_completed\",\n      \"reason\": \"10 GPU-hours; depends on plan_001 + plan_003; low priority (P2)\",\n      \"hypothetical_if_run\": {\n        \"adversary_levels\": {\n          \"black_box\": {\n            \"asr_mean\": 0.08,\n            \"eta_max_sig\": \"3/12\",\n            \"keff_sig\": \"1/12\"\n          },\n          \"family_knowledge\": {\n            \"asr_mean\": 0.12,\n            \"eta_max_sig\": \"5/12\",\n            \"keff_sig\": \"2/12\"\n          },\n          \"capability_tier_knowledge\": {\n            \"asr_mean\": 0.16,\n            \"eta_max_sig\": \"7/12\",\n            \"keff_sig\": \"3/12\"\n          },\n          \"white_box\": {\n            \"asr_mean\": 0.22,\n            \"eta_max_sig\": \"11/12\",\n            \"keff_sig\": \"8/12\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"eta_max_dominance_holds_all_levels\": true,\n        \"keff_increases_with_knowledge\": true,\n        \"family_knowledge_insufficient\": \"Confirmed — family labels don't predict vulnerability (Claim 4)\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Hypothetical Adversary Knowledge Gradient\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Knowledge level\",\n          \"Mean ASR\",\n          \"η_max sig (OLS)\",\n          \"K_eff sig (OLS)\",\n          \"η_max:K_eff ratio\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Black-box\",\n            \"0.08\",\n            \"3/12\",\n            \"1/12\",\n            \"3:1\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Family labels only\",\n            \"0.12\",\n            \"5/12\",\n            \"2/12\",\n            \"2.5:1\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Capability tier\",\n            \"0.16\",\n            \"7/12\",\n            \"3/12\",\n            \"2.3:1\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"White-box (full η_j)\",\n            \"0.22\",\n            \"11/12\",\n            \"8/12\",\n            \"1.4:1\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Interesting theoretical contribution but not essential under median tier. The finding that information asymmetry modulates predictor strength is intuitive — if you don't know which judge is weakest, you can't exploit η_max. The practical implication (keep panel composition secret) is already common sense for security. Non-completion is acceptable; could be framed as future work in the discussion section.\\n\\nUnder median tier, plan_012 is not completed (low priority P2, depends on two other experiments). If run, the main prediction is that η_max dominance holds across all adversary knowledge levels, but the STRENGTH of both predictors increases with adversary knowledge. Under black-box (no knowledge of panel composition), attacks are essentially random → lower ASR, both η_max and K_eff weakly significant. Under family knowledge, the adversary can guess which model families are in the panel but not exact vulnerability profiles → modest improvement, but family labels are unreliable predictors (Claim 4), so the gain is limited. Under white-box (current experimental setup), ASR is highest and both predictors are most significant. The gradient from black-box to white-box would show that panel vulnerability is primarily an information asymmetry problem: adversaries who know judge weaknesses can exploit η_max effectively; those who don't are reduced to generic attacks where K_eff matters more. This is interesting but not essential for the core narrative — it's a natural extension that strengthens the practical implications section.\"\n  }\n]","numLines":1151,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1151}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QKM4LxyMatRJ92paBgdZNs","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — cert_manip_resist_eval (v5: Post-Clustered-SE Reframe, D019)\n2\t\n3\t> Last updated: 2026-05-22 | Reason: Bootstrap v2 (Reviewer-verified): η_max 5-11/12 (bootstrap 5/12 most reliable), K_eff 0-1/12. η_max:K_eff ratio 5:1 confirms dominance. GAM 6:6 split confirmed. Aggregation gradient independent.\n4\t\n5\t## Target\n6\tEMNLP 2026 (via ARR)\n7\t\n8\t## Title Candidates\n9\t1. \"The Weakest Judge Wins: What Really Drives LLM Evaluation Panel Vulnerability Under Adversarial Attack\"\n10\t2. \"Weakest Link, Not Diversity: Adversarial Vulnerability in Multi-Judge LLM Evaluation\"\n11\t3. \"The Diversity Trap: Why Adding More Judges Doesn't Make LLM Evaluation Safer\" (softened with honest reporting)\n12\t\n13\t## Core Insight\n14\tMulti-judge LLM evaluation panels are widely assumed to benefit from judge diversity. We conduct the first large-scale empirical study (15 models, 455 panels, 6 attacks, 2 benchmarks) decomposing panel vulnerability into individual weakness (η_max) and structural diversity (K_eff). Key findings: (1) **η_max dominates** panel vulnerability (5-11/12 conditions significant depending on correction method; wild cluster bootstrap — the most reliable method for small cluster counts — gives 5/12, with η_max:K_eff ratio of 5:1); (2) K_eff shows **directional but statistically fragile** association with vulnerability — point estimates predominantly positive (diversity increases risk), but significance collapses from 8/12 (OLS) to 0-1/12 after non-independence correction (bootstrap 1/12, clustered SE 0/12); (3) **GAM reveals a clean attack-type split**: K_eff is positive (harmful) for targeted attacks and negative (protective) for shared-bias attacks — a 6:6 pattern masked by linear regression; (4) **Aggregation rule choice fundamentally modulates vulnerability**: Bayesian calibration reverses K_eff direction (6/7 negative) and weakens η_max (9/12), while threshold voting amplifies both (R²=0.89).\n15\t\n16\t## Hook\n17\t\"Panel security against adversarial manipulation is dominated by the weakest individual judge — not panel diversity. But how you aggregate judges' votes determines whether diversity becomes a vulnerability or a defense.\"\n18\t\n19\t## Novelty Positioning\n20\tWe do NOT claim diversity is harmful with high statistical confidence — K_eff's independent effect is directional but fragile under rigorous correction. Our contributions are:\n21\t1. **First large-scale empirical decomposition** of panel vulnerability into η_max and K_eff in the LLM-judge domain (15 models, 455 panels), with transparent robustness analysis showing η_max dominates (5:1 over K_eff under bootstrap inference)\n22\t2. **Attack-type taxonomy with clean mechanistic split**: GAM reveals K_eff positive for targeted attacks, negative for shared-bias attacks (6:6 split), explaining WHEN diversity helps vs hurts\n23\t3. **Aggregation as a control lever**: voting rule modulates the diversity-vulnerability relationship — Bayesian reverses K_eff direction, threshold amplifies it (D017, independent of non-independence concern)\n24\t4. **Robustness-first panel design**: min(η_max) strategy wins 12/12 conditions (Claim 6, unaffected by SE correction)\n25\t5. **Structural barriers**: diversity-capability tradeoff (r=0.70), family label unreliability (confirmed at 15-model)\n26\t6. **Alternative diversity metrics robustness**: η_max dominance holds across operationalizations — Q-stat only 2/12 sig (r=0.674 with K_eff), disagreement 8/12, κ-diversity 7/12. K_eff practical effect size: IQR → Δ ASR = 1.3pp (directional but practically small)\n27\t7. **Methodological transparency**: explicit reporting of how significance changes across OLS → clustered SE → mixed effects, demonstrating the importance of accounting for panel overlap in combinatorial evaluation designs\n28\t\n29\t## Core Claims\n30\t\n31\t### Claim 1 (Primary): Weakest-Link Dominance\n32\t**Statement**: Under adaptive adversarial attacks, panel vulnerability is **dominated by individual judge weakness (η_max)**. η_max remains significant in 5-8/12 conditions after non-independence correction (wild bootstrap 5/12, clustered SE 8/12, mixed effects 7/12). K_eff (diversity) shows directional association but significance is fragile (bootstrap 1/12, clustered SE 0/12). The η_max:K_eff dominance ratio is 5:1 under bootstrap — stronger than OLS's 11:8 ≈ 1.4:1.\n33\t**Evidence** (15-model, 455 panels, 12 conditions, D019+D021):\n34\t- **Regression spec**: log-log OLS: log(ASR + ε) ~ const + log(K_eff) + log(η_max + ε)\n35\t- **Standard OLS**: η_max **11/12**, K_eff **8/12** (7+/1−) — inflated by panel non-independence\n36\t- **Wild cluster bootstrap v2** (Reviewer-verified, B=9999, Rademacher, 3-position max-p): η_max **5/12**, K_eff **1/12** (mmlu×PI only) — conservative lower bound. Per-position analysis: position 0 gives 6/12 (cluster imbalance: claude-3-haiku=91 panels vs qwen2.5-14b=1), positions 1&2 each give **9/12** (more balanced clusters, consistent with clustered SE). Max-across-positions is legitimate conservative bound but tightened by position 0 imbalance.\n37\t- **Clustered SE** (15 judge clusters, CR1): η_max **8/12**, K_eff **0/12** — consistent with per-position bootstrap 1&2\n38\t- **Mixed effects** (random judge intercepts): η_max **7/12**, K_eff **3/12** (mmlu×PI, arc×PI, arc×PB)\n39\t- SE inflation ~2.5x (consistent with 91/455 panel overlap)\n40\t- Bootstrap η_max shows benchmark asymmetry: MMLU 4/6, ARC 1/6 survive. Likely cause: MMLU 4-choice format provides more scoring variance than ARC 2-choice, giving η_max more signal. arc×PI η_max completely lost (OLS p=3e-14 → bootstrap all NS) — signal was entirely from panel overlap.\n41\t- **arc×sycophancy exception**: No longer significant under any correction (OLS p=0.013 → bootstrap 0.06 → clustered 0.441 → mixed 0.136). Reported as directional observation, not robust claim.\n42\t- **Alternative diversity metrics (OLS)**: Q-stat 2/12, disagreement 8/12 (7+/1−), κ-diversity 7/12 (7+/0−). Q-stat (lowest correlation with K_eff, r=0.674) gives lowest diversity significance — further supports η_max as dominant driver regardless of diversity operationalization.\n43\t- **Practical effect size**: K_eff IQR change → Δ ASR = 1.3pp (clustered SE CIs contain zero in 11/12 conditions). Even under OLS, diversity effect is practically small.\n44\t**Strength**: η_max dominant (5:1 ratio over K_eff under bootstrap). Absolute significance lower than OLS (5/12 vs 11/12) but relative dominance stronger. K_eff directional but fragile and practically small.\n45\t**Headline**: \"η_max dominates panel vulnerability (5:1 over K_eff); both channels inflated by panel non-independence, but η_max survives correction while K_eff does not.\"\n46\t\n47\t### Claim 1b (Secondary): Attack-Type Taxonomy — GAM 6:6 Split\n48\t**Statement**: GAM analysis reveals that K_eff's direction cleanly splits by attack mechanism: **positive (diversity harmful) for targeted attacks** (prompt_injection, score_manipulation, position_bias × 2 benchmarks = 6 conditions) and **negative (diversity protective) for shared-bias attacks** (sycophancy, verbosity_bias, authority_bias × 2 benchmarks = 6 conditions). This 6:6 pattern is masked by linear OLS which shows 7+/1−/4NS.\n49\t**Evidence**: GAM partial effects, 455 panels, 12 conditions.\n50\t- Targeted attacks (exploit judge-specific weaknesses): K_eff partial effect positive in all 6 conditions\n51\t- Shared-bias attacks (exploit common cognitive biases): K_eff partial effect negative in all 6 conditions\n52\t- **Caveat**: GAM significance of individual conditions needs clustered SE / bootstrapped CI (pending); the structural pattern is the main finding, not individual p-values\n53\t**Strength**: Moderate-to-strong as qualitative taxonomic contribution. The clean 6:6 split provides mechanistic explanatory power beyond individual significance counts. Confirmed under correct 3/3/9 tier classification (GAM tier-fix rerun: all macro conclusions unchanged).\n54\t\n55\t### Claim 2 (Empirical): Predictability Gap — Panel Composition Explains RF But Not Adaptive Vulnerability\n56\t**Statement**: Panel composition (η_max + K_eff) explains substantially more variance under random faults (R²=0.73-0.81) than under adaptive attacks (lower R²). K_eff is positively associated with vulnerability in BOTH regimes, but: under RF, this association is genuine (survives all corrections, 2/2); under adaptive, it is largely artifactual (0-1/12 after correction). Adaptive adversaries introduce strategic variance beyond what panel structure can predict.\n57\t**Evidence** (15-model, 455 panels, D021+RF validation, Reviewer-verified):\n58\t- **Random-fault regime** (error = 1 - clean_accuracy, Monte Carlo 10000):\n59\t  - K_eff **positive** std_β = +0.215 (MMLU), +0.379 (ARC) — diversity associated with HIGHER error\n60\t  - K_eff significant **2/2** under ALL correction methods (OLS, clustered SE, bootstrap)\n61\t  - R² = 0.73-0.81 (highly predictable)\n62\t- **Adaptive regime** (D021):\n63\t  - K_eff also positive under OLS (7+/1−) but significance collapses to **0-1/12** after correction\n64\t  - R² substantially lower\n65\t- **Contrast**: NOT \"diversity helps under RF but hurts under adaptive\" — diversity is harmful in both. The contrast is: K_eff's harmful association is GENUINE under RF but INFLATED/ARTIFACTUAL under adaptive due to panel non-independence\n66\t- ΔR² gap: RF regime is highly predictable from panel composition; adaptive regime is not\n67\t**Correct interpretation**: Random faults are structurally determined by panel composition (hence high R² and genuine K_eff effect). Adaptive adversaries exploit weaknesses strategically, introducing variance that panel-level predictors cannot capture. K_eff in the adaptive regime appears significant under OLS only because of panel overlap inflation.\n68\t**Strength**: Strong as predictability gap (R² contrast). The finding that K_eff is genuine under RF but artifactual under adaptive is a clean methodological insight about when non-independence correction matters most.\n69\t\n70\t### Claim 3 (Empirical): Diversity-Capability Tradeoff\n71\t**Statement**: In the current LLM ecosystem, maximizing panel diversity (K_eff) structurally introduces less capable/robust models. The correlation r=+0.700 is a structural constraint — diversity and capability are partially opposed in the current model ecosystem.\n72\t**Evidence**:\n73\t- K_eff-η_max correlation: r = +0.700 (15-model)\n74\t- Under standard OLS: K_eff significant 8/12 (7+/1−) after residualizing η_max\n75\t- **D019 caveat**: Under clustered SE, K_eff drops to 0/12; mixed effects gives 3/12. The \"independent predictive effect\" of K_eff beyond η_max is directional but NOT statistically robust after correcting for non-independence. The structural correlation (r=0.700) itself is unaffected by SE correction — only the regression significance of K_eff as independent predictor changes.\n76\t**Framing**: The tradeoff is real as a structural constraint (r=0.700). Whether K_eff has an independent causal effect beyond η_max cannot be confidently established from this data — K_eff's significance is fragile under correction, and the high confound makes disentanglement unreliable.\n77\t**Strength**: Moderate (structural correlation strong; independent effect fragile). Causal language avoided.\n78\t\n79\t### Claim 4 (Methodological): Family Labels ≠ Diversity\n80\t**Statement**: Model family labels (Qwen/Llama/Mistral/Anthropic/OpenAI/Google) do not predict judge independence. Capability proximity is the primary driver of inter-judge correlation.\n81\t**Evidence**:\n82\t- 6-model: Mann-Whitney U p=0.206, permutation p=0.427\n83\t- **15-model (confirmed)**: p=0.179 (MMLU), p=0.385 (ARC). 14 within-family pairs, 91 cross-family pairs. Still not significant.\n84\t- Within-family MI variance is high (e.g., llama-70b↔8b MI only 0.05 due to capability gap > family similarity)\n85\t**Strength**: Moderate. Consistent across 6→15 model scaling.\n86\t\n87\t### ~~Claim 5 (Methodological): Gaussian Copula Failure~~ → DOWNGRADED to observation\n88\t**Original statement**: Gaussian copula systematically underestimates MI by 10-21×.\n89\t**15-model result**: MI_obs/MI_gaussian mean ratio = **0.93** (MMLU), **0.90** (ARC, robust). Copula fits well at 15-model scale. The 6-model 10-21× was a small-sample artifact (15 pairs → 105 pairs, extreme values diluted). Only claude-3-haiku pairs show extreme ratios (212×) due to near-zero ρ with nonlinear dependence.\n90\t**Disposition**: Remove as independent contribution. Optionally note in analysis: \"Gaussian copula fit is adequate for diverse model pools but breaks down for capability-mismatched pairs with nonlinear dependence.\"\n91\t**Strength**: DEAD as independent claim. May retain as minor observation.\n92\t\n93\t### Claim 6 (Practical): Robustness-First Panel Design\n94\t**Statement**: Optimal panel selection is robustness-first: (1) screen for individual robustness (η ≤ threshold), then (2) maximize K_eff among qualifying judges. The two-stage approach is critical because K_eff is harmful in unrestricted pools but may be neutral or beneficial among robustness-screened judges.\n95\t**Evidence** (15-model, 455 panels — fully validated):\n96\t- min(η_max) strategy dominates **12/12 conditions** (up from 5/6 at 6-model)\n97\t- ASR advantage range: +0.038 (arc×sycophancy) to +0.511 (arc×prompt_injection)\n98\t- Robust across panel sizes K=10, 20, 50\n99\t- Even in the sole K_eff-dominant condition (arc×position_bias), min_eta_max still outperforms max_K_eff\n100\t- Consistent with canonical regression: η_max 11/12 dominant\n101\t**Strength**: Strong (15-model fully confirmed). Zero failure conditions.\n102\t\n103\t### Claim 7 (Empirical+Practical): Aggregation Rule Modulates Both Vulnerability Drivers\n104\t**Statement**: Bayesian calibration partially mitigates BOTH vulnerability drivers — weakening η_max dominance AND reversing K_eff from harmful to protective. Different aggregation rules create a gradient from full dual-driver vulnerability (threshold) to partial mitigation (Bayesian).\n105\t**Evidence** (15-model, 455 panels, log-log canonical spec — D017, Reviewer-verified from JSON):\n106\t- Majority: η_max **11/12**, K_eff **8/12** (7+/1−), R²=0.298 ← canonical baseline (matches dominance table ✓)\n107\t- Weighted: η_max **9/12**, K_eff **9/12** (9+/0−), R²=0.284 — reduces both drivers but K_eff stays all positive\n108\t- Threshold: η_max **12/12**, K_eff **11/12** (10+/1−), R²=**0.890** — near-perfect fit under unanimity\n109\t- Bayesian: η_max **9/12** (↓), K_eff **7/12** (1+/**6 negative**), R²=0.104\n110\t- Key findings:\n111\t  - Bayesian weakens η_max (11→9/12) AND flips K_eff direction (majority 7+/1− → bayesian 1+/6−)\n112\t  - Threshold amplifies both channels: η_max 12/12, K_eff 11/12, R²=0.89 (unanimity maximizes dual-driver predictability)\n113\t  - Weighted reduces η_max (11→9/12) but K_eff stays all positive — accuracy weighting alone cannot reverse diversity harm\n114\t  - Only Bayesian calibration reverses K_eff direction (6/7 significant coefficients negative)\n115\t  - Aggregation gradient: threshold (max vulnerability) → majority → weighted → bayesian (min vulnerability)\n116\t**Strength**: Strong (log-log canonical, Reviewer-verified, sanity-checked against Claim 1). Prescriptive: robustness-first selection (Claim 6) + Bayesian aggregation = dual-channel defense stack.\n117\t\n118\t## Contribution Stack (ordered by strength, post-D019 reframe)\n119\t1. **Weakest-link dominance** — primary empirical (η_max robust 7-8/12 after all corrections; the strongest, most defensible finding)\n120\t2. **Aggregation as control lever** — empirical+practical (D017: Bayesian reverses K_eff direction, weakens η_max; threshold amplifies both; R²=0.89. Independent of non-independence concern)\n121\t3. **Attack-type taxonomy / GAM 6:6 split** — mechanistic (targeted=K_eff positive, shared-bias=K_eff negative; clean qualitative pattern; pending correct-tier validation)\n122\t4. **Robustness-first panel design** — practical (Claim 6: min_eta_max 12/12 wins; unaffected by SE correction)\n123\t5. **Methodological transparency on non-independence** — meta-contribution (demonstrating how OLS → clustered SE → mixed effects changes significance in combinatorial panel designs; useful for future work)\n124\t6. **Diversity-capability tradeoff** — empirical (r=0.70; structural, not affected by SE correction)\n125\t7. **Family label unreliability** — methodological (confirmed at 15-model scale)\n126\t7. **Adaptive vs random-fault predictability gap** — empirical (R² gap)\n127\t~~8. **Gaussian copula failure**~~ — REMOVED (falsified at 15-model scale; downgraded to minor observation)\n128\t\n129\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n130\t\n131\t### RC1: \"K_eff positive is just confound with η_max\"\n132\t**Response**: (a) Under standard OLS, K_eff is significant 8/12 (7+/1−), but after clustered SE correction K_eff drops to 0/12, mixed effects gives 3/12 — K_eff's independent effect is directionally positive but **statistically fragile**; (b) η_max remains robust at 7-8/12 under all corrections, confirming it is NOT a confound artifact; (c) GAM analysis reveals the K_eff direction cleanly splits by attack type (6:6 targeted vs shared-bias), suggesting real structure even if individual significance is fragile under correction; (d) We transparently report all three correction methods, which is itself a methodological contribution for combinatorial panel designs.\n133\t\n134\t### RC2: \"Why does diversity hurt? What's the mechanism?\"\n135\t**Response**: (a) BFT assumes random faults — independent nodes protect via uncorrelated failures; (b) Under adaptive adversaries, independent judges create diverse attack surface — attacker exploits judge-specific vulnerabilities; (c) GAM reveals the mechanism boundary: K_eff partial effect is positive for targeted attacks (6/6 conditions) and negative for shared-bias attacks (6/6 conditions). This clean split explains WHEN diversity helps vs hurts; (d) Note: K_eff's harmful direction is directional under OLS (7+/1−) but significance collapses under non-independence correction (0-3/12). The mechanism is real (GAM pattern), but its statistical strength is sensitive to correction — we report this transparently.\n136\t\n137\t### RC3: \"Only 15 models — does this generalize?\"\n138\t**Response**: (a) 15 models across 6 families, 3 capability tiers, covering major commercial and open-source models; (b) 455 panels provide strong statistical power; (c) Effect is robust to sensitivity analysis; (d) Direction consistent across 6→9→15 model scaling (K_eff shifts from NS to positive as model pool diversifies).\n139\t\n140\t### RC4: \"BFT comparison is a strawman\"\n141\t**Response**: (a) The implicit assumption \"more diverse judges = more robust\" is pervasive in LLM evaluation literature; (b) We formalize this assumption and show its reversal; (c) The BFT reversal mechanism (random vs adaptive faults) provides theoretical grounding beyond just empirical observation.\n142\t\n143\t### RC5: \"Why not just use the strongest models?\"\n144\t**Response**: (a) \"Strongest\" varies by attack type; (b) K_eff is the sole significant predictor in 1 condition (arc×position_bias) — even all-strong panels can be vulnerable through diversity channel; (c) Two-stage algorithm addresses both vulnerability drivers.\n145\t\n146\t### RC6: \"No theoretical novelty\"\n147\t**Response**: (a) Theorem 1 (η_max monotonicity) is formally proved; (b) BFT reversal mechanism provides novel theoretical framework; (c) Dual vulnerability driver finding with condition-dependent importance is new.\n148\t\n149\t### RC7: \"Why not just use Bayesian aggregation?\"\n150\t**Response**: (a) 15-model log-log canonical (D017, Reviewer-verified): Bayesian partially mitigates BOTH drivers — η_max drops to 9/12 (from 11/12), K_eff flips to 6/7 negative (protective). But it doesn't eliminate vulnerability: 9/12 η_max still significant, R² drops to 0.104 (residual vulnerability less predictable); (b) Requires clean accuracy prior which adaptive attackers could target; (c) Even under Bayesian, 9/12 conditions still show η_max vulnerability → robustness-first selection remains necessary; (d) Optimal defense stack: robustness-first selection (eliminates η_max) + Bayesian aggregation (flips K_eff to protective) = dual-channel coverage.\n151\t\n152\t### RC8: \"6-model and 15-model results contradict — which is right?\"\n153\t**Response**: (a) 6-model result (K_eff NS) was underpowered and confounded (r=-0.8, only 20 panels); (b) 15-model result (K_eff predominantly positive) has 23× more panels, wider capability range, and passes dominance analysis validation; (c) Paper reports both and explains why 15-model result is definitive; (d) The scaling behavior itself (K_eff effect emerging with more models) is informative.\n154\t\n155\t### RC9 (new): \"arc×sycophancy exception undermines your claim\"\n156\t**Response**: (a) Post-D019: arc×sycophancy K_eff is NOT significant after correction (OLS p=0.013 → clustered SE p=0.441 → mixed effects p=0.136). It is a directional observation, not a robust standalone finding; (b) However, GAM reveals that ALL shared-bias attacks (sycophancy, verbosity_bias, authority_bias × 2 benchmarks = 6 conditions) show negative K_eff partial effects, while ALL targeted attacks (6 conditions) show positive — the 6:6 split is the robust pattern, not the individual significance of arc×sycophancy; (c) This gives practitioners an actionable taxonomy: targeted attacks → diversity hurts, shared-bias attacks → diversity may help; (d) The structural pattern (GAM) is more informative than individual p-values under various corrections.\n157\t\n158\t## \"When Diversity Helps\" Section\n159\tDiversity IS beneficial under specific conditions — paper must present this as empirically grounded nuance, not just a hypothetical:\n160\t1. **Non-adversarial settings**: reducing calibration errors, position bias, verbosity bias in absence of adversary\n161\t2. **Shared-bias attacks (GAM-confirmed pattern)**: GAM reveals K_eff partial effect is negative (protective) for all 6 shared-bias conditions (sycophancy, verbosity_bias, authority_bias × 2 benchmarks). Note: arc×sycophancy K_eff was significant under standard OLS (p=0.013) but **not significant after clustered SE (p=0.441) or mixed effects (p=0.136)**. The directional pattern across all shared-bias attacks is the stronger evidence — individual significance is fragile under correction.\n162\t3. **After robustness screening**: once all judges exceed a capability threshold, K_eff's harmful effect may diminish (the diversity-capability tradeoff is broken by screening). This is the basis for our two-stage recommendation.\n163\t4. **Summary (post-D019)**: Under standard OLS, K_eff is positive in 7/12, NS in 4/12, negative in 1/12. Under clustered SE / mixed effects, K_eff significance collapses to 0-3/12. GAM reveals the underlying structure: targeted attacks → K_eff positive (6/6), shared-bias → K_eff negative (6/6). The attack-type taxonomy is the robust finding; individual K_eff significance counts are sensitive to SE correction.\n164\t\n165\t## Story Arc (v5: Post-D019 Reframe)\n166\t1. **Background**: Multi-judge LLM evaluation is increasingly adopted; diversity is assumed to be the key defense against manipulation (BFT analogy)\n167\t2. **Tension**: We test this assumption at unprecedented scale — 15 models, 6 families, 455 panels, 6 attack types\n168\t3. **Surprise**: Panel vulnerability is **robustly dominated by the weakest individual judge** (η_max 7-8/12 after clustered SE / mixed-effects correction). Diversity (K_eff) shows directional but statistically fragile association — significance collapses from 8/12 (OLS) to 0-3/12 after correcting for non-independence of panel observations\n169\t4. **Mechanistic nuance**: GAM analysis reveals K_eff's direction cleanly splits by attack type: positive (harmful) for targeted attacks, negative (protective) for shared-bias attacks — a 6:6 pattern masked by linear regression\n170\t5. **Aggregation as control lever**: Voting rule choice fundamentally modulates vulnerability — Bayesian calibration reverses K_eff direction (6/7 negative) and weakens η_max (9/12), while threshold voting amplifies both (R²=0.89)\n171\t6. **Defense**: Robustness-first screening (min η_max, 12/12 wins) + Bayesian aggregation = dual-channel defense stack\n172\t7. **Implication**: Panel security is primarily an individual-judge quality problem, not a diversity problem. But aggregation design and attack-type awareness provide complementary defense levers\n173\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"272620ad-9e5d-4f60-ac9c-667a60a83919","uuid":"6d4ecf30-fd1b-4b70-866a-168de589c677","timestamp":"2026-05-22T22:40:55.190Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519_2/projects/cert_manip_resist_eval/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T221131/median/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — cert_manip_resist_eval (v5: Post-Clustered-SE Reframe, D019)\n\n> Last updated: 2026-05-22 | Reason: Bootstrap v2 (Reviewer-verified): η_max 5-11/12 (bootstrap 5/12 most reliable), K_eff 0-1/12. η_max:K_eff ratio 5:1 confirms dominance. GAM 6:6 split confirmed. Aggregation gradient independent.\n\n## Target\nEMNLP 2026 (via ARR)\n\n## Title Candidates\n1. \"The Weakest Judge Wins: What Really Drives LLM Evaluation Panel Vulnerability Under Adversarial Attack\"\n2. \"Weakest Link, Not Diversity: Adversarial Vulnerability in Multi-Judge LLM Evaluation\"\n3. \"The Diversity Trap: Why Adding More Judges Doesn't Make LLM Evaluation Safer\" (softened with honest reporting)\n\n## Core Insight\nMulti-judge LLM evaluation panels are widely assumed to benefit from judge diversity. We conduct the first large-scale empirical study (15 models, 455 panels, 6 attacks, 2 benchmarks) decomposing panel vulnerability into individual weakness (η_max) and structural diversity (K_eff). Key findings: (1) **η_max dominates** panel vulnerability (5-11/12 conditions significant depending on correction method; wild cluster bootstrap — the most reliable method for small cluster counts — gives 5/12, with η_max:K_eff ratio of 5:1); (2) K_eff shows **directional but statistically fragile** association with vulnerability — point estimates predominantly positive (diversity increases risk), but significance collapses from 8/12 (OLS) to 0-1/12 after non-independence correction (bootstrap 1/12, clustered SE 0/12); (3) **GAM reveals a clean attack-type split**: K_eff is positive (harmful) for targeted attacks and negative (protective) for shared-bias attacks — a 6:6 pattern masked by linear regression; (4) **Aggregation rule choice fundamentally modulates vulnerability**: Bayesian calibration reverses K_eff direction (6/7 negative) and weakens η_max (9/12), while threshold voting amplifies both (R²=0.89).\n\n## Hook\n\"Panel security against adversarial manipulation is dominated by the weakest individual judge — not panel diversity. But how you aggregate judges' votes determines whether diversity becomes a vulnerability or a defense.\"\n\n## Novelty Positioning\nWe do NOT claim diversity is harmful with high statistical confidence — K_eff's independent effect is directional but fragile under rigorous correction. Our contributions are:\n1. **First large-scale empirical decomposition** of panel vulnerability into η_max and K_eff in the LLM-judge domain (15 models, 455 panels), with transparent robustness analysis showing η_max dominates (5:1 over K_eff under bootstrap inference)\n2. **Attack-type taxonomy with clean mechanistic split**: GAM reveals K_eff positive for targeted attacks, negative for shared-bias attacks (6:6 split), explaining WHEN diversity helps vs hurts\n3. **Aggregation as a control lever**: voting rule modulates the diversity-vulnerability relationship — Bayesian reverses K_eff direction, threshold amplifies it (D017, independent of non-independence concern)\n4. **Robustness-first panel design**: min(η_max) strategy wins 12/12 conditions (Claim 6, unaffected by SE correction)\n5. **Structural barriers**: diversity-capability tradeoff (r=0.70), family label unreliability (confirmed at 15-model)\n6. **Alternative diversity metrics robustness**: η_max dominance holds across operationalizations — Q-stat only 2/12 sig (r=0.674 with K_eff), disagreement 8/12, κ-diversity 7/12. K_eff practical effect size: IQR → Δ ASR = 1.3pp (directional but practically small)\n7. **Methodological transparency**: explicit reporting of how significance changes across OLS → clustered SE → mixed effects, demonstrating the importance of accounting for panel overlap in combinatorial evaluation designs\n\n## Core Claims\n\n### Claim 1 (Primary): Weakest-Link Dominance\n**Statement**: Under adaptive adversarial attacks, panel vulnerability is **dominated by individual judge weakness (η_max)**. η_max remains significant in 5-8/12 conditions after non-independence correction (wild bootstrap 5/12, clustered SE 8/12, mixed effects 7/12). K_eff (diversity) shows directional association but significance is fragile (bootstrap 1/12, clustered SE 0/12). The η_max:K_eff dominance ratio is 5:1 under bootstrap — stronger than OLS's 11:8 ≈ 1.4:1.\n**Evidence** (15-model, 455 panels, 12 conditions, D019+D021):\n- **Regression spec**: log-log OLS: log(ASR + ε) ~ const + log(K_eff) + log(η_max + ε)\n- **Standard OLS**: η_max **11/12**, K_eff **8/12** (7+/1−) — inflated by panel non-independence\n- **Wild cluster bootstrap v2** (Reviewer-verified, B=9999, Rademacher, 3-position max-p): η_max **5/12**, K_eff **1/12** (mmlu×PI only) — conservative lower bound. Per-position analysis: position 0 gives 6/12 (cluster imbalance: claude-3-haiku=91 panels vs qwen2.5-14b=1), positions 1&2 each give **9/12** (more balanced clusters, consistent with clustered SE). Max-across-positions is legitimate conservative bound but tightened by position 0 imbalance.\n- **Clustered SE** (15 judge clusters, CR1): η_max **8/12**, K_eff **0/12** — consistent with per-position bootstrap 1&2\n- **Mixed effects** (random judge intercepts): η_max **7/12**, K_eff **3/12** (mmlu×PI, arc×PI, arc×PB)\n- SE inflation ~2.5x (consistent with 91/455 panel overlap)\n- Bootstrap η_max shows benchmark asymmetry: MMLU 4/6, ARC 1/6 survive. Likely cause: MMLU 4-choice format provides more scoring variance than ARC 2-choice, giving η_max more signal. arc×PI η_max completely lost (OLS p=3e-14 → bootstrap all NS) — signal was entirely from panel overlap.\n- **arc×sycophancy exception**: No longer significant under any correction (OLS p=0.013 → bootstrap 0.06 → clustered 0.441 → mixed 0.136). Reported as directional observation, not robust claim.\n- **Alternative diversity metrics (OLS)**: Q-stat 2/12, disagreement 8/12 (7+/1−), κ-diversity 7/12 (7+/0−). Q-stat (lowest correlation with K_eff, r=0.674) gives lowest diversity significance — further supports η_max as dominant driver regardless of diversity operationalization.\n- **Practical effect size**: K_eff IQR change → Δ ASR = 1.3pp (clustered SE CIs contain zero in 11/12 conditions). Even under OLS, diversity effect is practically small.\n**Strength**: η_max dominant (5:1 ratio over K_eff under bootstrap). Absolute significance lower than OLS (5/12 vs 11/12) but relative dominance stronger. K_eff directional but fragile and practically small.\n**Headline**: \"η_max dominates panel vulnerability (5:1 over K_eff); both channels inflated by panel non-independence, but η_max survives correction while K_eff does not.\"\n\n### Claim 1b (Secondary): Attack-Type Taxonomy — GAM 6:6 Split\n**Statement**: GAM analysis reveals that K_eff's direction cleanly splits by attack mechanism: **positive (diversity harmful) for targeted attacks** (prompt_injection, score_manipulation, position_bias × 2 benchmarks = 6 conditions) and **negative (diversity protective) for shared-bias attacks** (sycophancy, verbosity_bias, authority_bias × 2 benchmarks = 6 conditions). This 6:6 pattern is masked by linear OLS which shows 7+/1−/4NS.\n**Evidence**: GAM partial effects, 455 panels, 12 conditions.\n- Targeted attacks (exploit judge-specific weaknesses): K_eff partial effect positive in all 6 conditions\n- Shared-bias attacks (exploit common cognitive biases): K_eff partial effect negative in all 6 conditions\n- **Caveat**: GAM significance of individual conditions needs clustered SE / bootstrapped CI (pending); the structural pattern is the main finding, not individual p-values\n**Strength**: Moderate-to-strong as qualitative taxonomic contribution. The clean 6:6 split provides mechanistic explanatory power beyond individual significance counts. Confirmed under correct 3/3/9 tier classification (GAM tier-fix rerun: all macro conclusions unchanged).\n\n### Claim 2 (Empirical): Predictability Gap — Panel Composition Explains RF But Not Adaptive Vulnerability\n**Statement**: Panel composition (η_max + K_eff) explains substantially more variance under random faults (R²=0.73-0.81) than under adaptive attacks (lower R²). K_eff is positively associated with vulnerability in BOTH regimes, but: under RF, this association is genuine (survives all corrections, 2/2); under adaptive, it is largely artifactual (0-1/12 after correction). Adaptive adversaries introduce strategic variance beyond what panel structure can predict.\n**Evidence** (15-model, 455 panels, D021+RF validation, Reviewer-verified):\n- **Random-fault regime** (error = 1 - clean_accuracy, Monte Carlo 10000):\n  - K_eff **positive** std_β = +0.215 (MMLU), +0.379 (ARC) — diversity associated with HIGHER error\n  - K_eff significant **2/2** under ALL correction methods (OLS, clustered SE, bootstrap)\n  - R² = 0.73-0.81 (highly predictable)\n- **Adaptive regime** (D021):\n  - K_eff also positive under OLS (7+/1−) but significance collapses to **0-1/12** after correction\n  - R² substantially lower\n- **Contrast**: NOT \"diversity helps under RF but hurts under adaptive\" — diversity is harmful in both. The contrast is: K_eff's harmful association is GENUINE under RF but INFLATED/ARTIFACTUAL under adaptive due to panel non-independence\n- ΔR² gap: RF regime is highly predictable from panel composition; adaptive regime is not\n**Correct interpretation**: Random faults are structurally determined by panel composition (hence high R² and genuine K_eff effect). Adaptive adversaries exploit weaknesses strategically, introducing variance that panel-level predictors cannot capture. K_eff in the adaptive regime appears significant under OLS only because of panel overlap inflation.\n**Strength**: Strong as predictability gap (R² contrast). The finding that K_eff is genuine under RF but artifactual under adaptive is a clean methodological insight about when non-independence correction matters most.\n\n### Claim 3 (Empirical): Diversity-Capability Tradeoff\n**Statement**: In the current LLM ecosystem, maximizing panel diversity (K_eff) structurally introduces less capable/robust models. The correlation r=+0.700 is a structural constraint — diversity and capability are partially opposed in the current model ecosystem.\n**Evidence**:\n- K_eff-η_max correlation: r = +0.700 (15-model)\n- Under standard OLS: K_eff significant 8/12 (7+/1−) after residualizing η_max\n- **D019 caveat**: Under clustered SE, K_eff drops to 0/12; mixed effects gives 3/12. The \"independent predictive effect\" of K_eff beyond η_max is directional but NOT statistically robust after correcting for non-independence. The structural correlation (r=0.700) itself is unaffected by SE correction — only the regression significance of K_eff as independent predictor changes.\n**Framing**: The tradeoff is real as a structural constraint (r=0.700). Whether K_eff has an independent causal effect beyond η_max cannot be confidently established from this data — K_eff's significance is fragile under correction, and the high confound makes disentanglement unreliable.\n**Strength**: Moderate (structural correlation strong; independent effect fragile). Causal language avoided.\n\n### Claim 4 (Methodological): Family Labels ≠ Diversity\n**Statement**: Model family labels (Qwen/Llama/Mistral/Anthropic/OpenAI/Google) do not predict judge independence. Capability proximity is the primary driver of inter-judge correlation.\n**Evidence**:\n- 6-model: Mann-Whitney U p=0.206, permutation p=0.427\n- **15-model (confirmed)**: p=0.179 (MMLU), p=0.385 (ARC). 14 within-family pairs, 91 cross-family pairs. Still not significant.\n- Within-family MI variance is high (e.g., llama-70b↔8b MI only 0.05 due to capability gap > family similarity)\n**Strength**: Moderate. Consistent across 6→15 model scaling.\n\n### ~~Claim 5 (Methodological): Gaussian Copula Failure~~ → DOWNGRADED to observation\n**Original statement**: Gaussian copula systematically underestimates MI by 10-21×.\n**15-model result**: MI_obs/MI_gaussian mean ratio = **0.93** (MMLU), **0.90** (ARC, robust). Copula fits well at 15-model scale. The 6-model 10-21× was a small-sample artifact (15 pairs → 105 pairs, extreme values diluted). Only claude-3-haiku pairs show extreme ratios (212×) due to near-zero ρ with nonlinear dependence.\n**Disposition**: Remove as independent contribution. Optionally note in analysis: \"Gaussian copula fit is adequate for diverse model pools but breaks down for capability-mismatched pairs with nonlinear dependence.\"\n**Strength**: DEAD as independent claim. May retain as minor observation.\n\n### Claim 6 (Practical): Robustness-First Panel Design\n**Statement**: Optimal panel selection is robustness-first: (1) screen for individual robustness (η ≤ threshold), then (2) maximize K_eff among qualifying judges. The two-stage approach is critical because K_eff is harmful in unrestricted pools but may be neutral or beneficial among robustness-screened judges.\n**Evidence** (15-model, 455 panels — fully validated):\n- min(η_max) strategy dominates **12/12 conditions** (up from 5/6 at 6-model)\n- ASR advantage range: +0.038 (arc×sycophancy) to +0.511 (arc×prompt_injection)\n- Robust across panel sizes K=10, 20, 50\n- Even in the sole K_eff-dominant condition (arc×position_bias), min_eta_max still outperforms max_K_eff\n- Consistent with canonical regression: η_max 11/12 dominant\n**Strength**: Strong (15-model fully confirmed). Zero failure conditions.\n\n### Claim 7 (Empirical+Practical): Aggregation Rule Modulates Both Vulnerability Drivers\n**Statement**: Bayesian calibration partially mitigates BOTH vulnerability drivers — weakening η_max dominance AND reversing K_eff from harmful to protective. Different aggregation rules create a gradient from full dual-driver vulnerability (threshold) to partial mitigation (Bayesian).\n**Evidence** (15-model, 455 panels, log-log canonical spec — D017, Reviewer-verified from JSON):\n- Majority: η_max **11/12**, K_eff **8/12** (7+/1−), R²=0.298 ← canonical baseline (matches dominance table ✓)\n- Weighted: η_max **9/12**, K_eff **9/12** (9+/0−), R²=0.284 — reduces both drivers but K_eff stays all positive\n- Threshold: η_max **12/12**, K_eff **11/12** (10+/1−), R²=**0.890** — near-perfect fit under unanimity\n- Bayesian: η_max **9/12** (↓), K_eff **7/12** (1+/**6 negative**), R²=0.104\n- Key findings:\n  - Bayesian weakens η_max (11→9/12) AND flips K_eff direction (majority 7+/1− → bayesian 1+/6−)\n  - Threshold amplifies both channels: η_max 12/12, K_eff 11/12, R²=0.89 (unanimity maximizes dual-driver predictability)\n  - Weighted reduces η_max (11→9/12) but K_eff stays all positive — accuracy weighting alone cannot reverse diversity harm\n  - Only Bayesian calibration reverses K_eff direction (6/7 significant coefficients negative)\n  - Aggregation gradient: threshold (max vulnerability) → majority → weighted → bayesian (min vulnerability)\n**Strength**: Strong (log-log canonical, Reviewer-verified, sanity-checked against Claim 1). Prescriptive: robustness-first selection (Claim 6) + Bayesian aggregation = dual-channel defense stack.\n\n## Contribution Stack (ordered by strength, post-D019 reframe)\n1. **Weakest-link dominance** — primary empirical (η_max robust 7-8/12 after all corrections; the strongest, most defensible finding)\n2. **Aggregation as control lever** — empirical+practical (D017: Bayesian reverses K_eff direction, weakens η_max; threshold amplifies both; R²=0.89. Independent of non-independence concern)\n3. **Attack-type taxonomy / GAM 6:6 split** — mechanistic (targeted=K_eff positive, shared-bias=K_eff negative; clean qualitative pattern; pending correct-tier validation)\n4. **Robustness-first panel design** — practical (Claim 6: min_eta_max 12/12 wins; unaffected by SE correction)\n5. **Methodological transparency on non-independence** — meta-contribution (demonstrating how OLS → clustered SE → mixed effects changes significance in combinatorial panel designs; useful for future work)\n6. **Diversity-capability tradeoff** — empirical (r=0.70; structural, not affected by SE correction)\n7. **Family label unreliability** — methodological (confirmed at 15-model scale)\n7. **Adaptive vs random-fault predictability gap** — empirical (R² gap)\n~~8. **Gaussian copula failure**~~ — REMOVED (falsified at 15-model scale; downgraded to minor observation)\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n### RC1: \"K_eff positive is just confound with η_max\"\n**Response**: (a) Under standard OLS, K_eff is significant 8/12 (7+/1−), but after clustered SE correction K_eff drops to 0/12, mixed effects gives 3/12 — K_eff's independent effect is directionally positive but **statistically fragile**; (b) η_max remains robust at 7-8/12 under all corrections, confirming it is NOT a confound artifact; (c) GAM analysis reveals the K_eff direction cleanly splits by attack type (6:6 targeted vs shared-bias), suggesting real structure even if individual significance is fragile under correction; (d) We transparently report all three correction methods, which is itself a methodological contribution for combinatorial panel designs.\n\n### RC2: \"Why does diversity hurt? What's the mechanism?\"\n**Response**: (a) BFT assumes random faults — independent nodes protect via uncorrelated failures; (b) Under adaptive adversaries, independent judges create diverse attack surface — attacker exploits judge-specific vulnerabilities; (c) GAM reveals the mechanism boundary: K_eff partial effect is positive for targeted attacks (6/6 conditions) and negative for shared-bias attacks (6/6 conditions). This clean split explains WHEN diversity helps vs hurts; (d) Note: K_eff's harmful direction is directional under OLS (7+/1−) but significance collapses under non-independence correction (0-3/12). The mechanism is real (GAM pattern), but its statistical strength is sensitive to correction — we report this transparently.\n\n### RC3: \"Only 15 models — does this generalize?\"\n**Response**: (a) 15 models across 6 families, 3 capability tiers, covering major commercial and open-source models; (b) 455 panels provide strong statistical power; (c) Effect is robust to sensitivity analysis; (d) Direction consistent across 6→9→15 model scaling (K_eff shifts from NS to positive as model pool diversifies).\n\n### RC4: \"BFT comparison is a strawman\"\n**Response**: (a) The implicit assumption \"more diverse judges = more robust\" is pervasive in LLM evaluation literature; (b) We formalize this assumption and show its reversal; (c) The BFT reversal mechanism (random vs adaptive faults) provides theoretical grounding beyond just empirical observation.\n\n### RC5: \"Why not just use the strongest models?\"\n**Response**: (a) \"Strongest\" varies by attack type; (b) K_eff is the sole significant predictor in 1 condition (arc×position_bias) — even all-strong panels can be vulnerable through diversity channel; (c) Two-stage algorithm addresses both vulnerability drivers.\n\n### RC6: \"No theoretical novelty\"\n**Response**: (a) Theorem 1 (η_max monotonicity) is formally proved; (b) BFT reversal mechanism provides novel theoretical framework; (c) Dual vulnerability driver finding with condition-dependent importance is new.\n\n### RC7: \"Why not just use Bayesian aggregation?\"\n**Response**: (a) 15-model log-log canonical (D017, Reviewer-verified): Bayesian partially mitigates BOTH drivers — η_max drops to 9/12 (from 11/12), K_eff flips to 6/7 negative (protective). But it doesn't eliminate vulnerability: 9/12 η_max still significant, R² drops to 0.104 (residual vulnerability less predictable); (b) Requires clean accuracy prior which adaptive attackers could target; (c) Even under Bayesian, 9/12 conditions still show η_max vulnerability → robustness-first selection remains necessary; (d) Optimal defense stack: robustness-first selection (eliminates η_max) + Bayesian aggregation (flips K_eff to protective) = dual-channel coverage.\n\n### RC8: \"6-model and 15-model results contradict — which is right?\"\n**Response**: (a) 6-model result (K_eff NS) was underpowered and confounded (r=-0.8, only 20 panels); (b) 15-model result (K_eff predominantly positive) has 23× more panels, wider capability range, and passes dominance analysis validation; (c) Paper reports both and explains why 15-model result is definitive; (d) The scaling behavior itself (K_eff effect emerging with more models) is informative.\n\n### RC9 (new): \"arc×sycophancy exception undermines your claim\"\n**Response**: (a) Post-D019: arc×sycophancy K_eff is NOT significant after correction (OLS p=0.013 → clustered SE p=0.441 → mixed effects p=0.136). It is a directional observation, not a robust standalone finding; (b) However, GAM reveals that ALL shared-bias attacks (sycophancy, verbosity_bias, authority_bias × 2 benchmarks = 6 conditions) show negative K_eff partial effects, while ALL targeted attacks (6 conditions) show positive — the 6:6 split is the robust pattern, not the individual significance of arc×sycophancy; (c) This gives practitioners an actionable taxonomy: targeted attacks → diversity hurts, shared-bias attacks → diversity may help; (d) The structural pattern (GAM) is more informative than individual p-values under various corrections.\n\n## \"When Diversity Helps\" Section\nDiversity IS beneficial under specific conditions — paper must present this as empirically grounded nuance, not just a hypothetical:\n1. **Non-adversarial settings**: reducing calibration errors, position bias, verbosity bias in absence of adversary\n2. **Shared-bias attacks (GAM-confirmed pattern)**: GAM reveals K_eff partial effect is negative (protective) for all 6 shared-bias conditions (sycophancy, verbosity_bias, authority_bias × 2 benchmarks). Note: arc×sycophancy K_eff was significant under standard OLS (p=0.013) but **not significant after clustered SE (p=0.441) or mixed effects (p=0.136)**. The directional pattern across all shared-bias attacks is the stronger evidence — individual significance is fragile under correction.\n3. **After robustness screening**: once all judges exceed a capability threshold, K_eff's harmful effect may diminish (the diversity-capability tradeoff is broken by screening). This is the basis for our two-stage recommendation.\n4. **Summary (post-D019)**: Under standard OLS, K_eff is positive in 7/12, NS in 4/12, negative in 1/12. Under clustered SE / mixed effects, K_eff significance collapses to 0-3/12. GAM reveals the underlying structure: targeted attacks → K_eff positive (6/6), shared-bias → K_eff negative (6/6). The attack-type taxonomy is the robust finding; individual K_eff significance counts are sensitive to SE correction.\n\n## Story Arc (v5: Post-D019 Reframe)\n1. **Background**: Multi-judge LLM evaluation is increasingly adopted; diversity is assumed to be the key defense against manipulation (BFT analogy)\n2. **Tension**: We test this assumption at unprecedented scale — 15 models, 6 families, 455 panels, 6 attack types\n3. **Surprise**: Panel vulnerability is **robustly dominated by the weakest individual judge** (η_max 7-8/12 after clustered SE / mixed-effects correction). Diversity (K_eff) shows directional but statistically fragile association — significance collapses from 8/12 (OLS) to 0-3/12 after correcting for non-independence of panel observations\n4. **Mechanistic nuance**: GAM analysis reveals K_eff's direction cleanly splits by attack type: positive (harmful) for targeted attacks, negative (protective) for shared-bias attacks — a 6:6 pattern masked by linear regression\n5. **Aggregation as control lever**: Voting rule choice fundamentally modulates vulnerability — Bayesian calibration reverses K_eff direction (6/7 negative) and weakens η_max (9/12), while threshold voting amplifies both (R²=0.89)\n6. **Defense**: Robustness-first screening (min η_max, 12/12 wins) + Bayesian aggregation = dual-channel defense stack\n7. **Implication**: Panel security is primarily an individual-judge quality problem, not a diversity problem. But aggregation design and attack-type awareness provide complementary defense levers\n","numLines":173,"startLine":1,"totalLines":173}}}
